    "ls|LS17_S9_U_107": "������������������� �\n������������������� �\n������������������������������ �\n�\n���������� �\n�������������������������� �\n������������������ ��������������\n�\n������� ���� �\n�������������������������� �\n�\n���������������������������� �����������������������\n�\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�����������������������\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������\n�\n������������������������������ �\n���������������������� ��\n�\n����� �������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n��������������������������������������������������������������\n���� � ���������������� � ������������������� �\n����� ���� ������������ ������������\n����� ���� ������������ ������������\n����� ���� ������������ ������������\n����� ���� ������������\n�\n������������\n����� ������������ � ������������ ������������\n����� �������� ���� � ������������ ������������\n���� ������������ � ������������ ������������\n���� ��������� ������������ ������������\n��������������������������������� �������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n���� �� ������� ���� ��������� ������������ �������������� ��������� ��� ����������� ���� ����������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���� ���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������ ���� ��������� ��������� ��� ���� �������� ���������� ��� ����� ���� ����� ����� ��������� ���\n�������������� �� ���� ����� ���� ������ ������� ����� ��������� �������� ���� ��������� ��� ������� ���\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������� ��� ����� ������� �������� ������� ������� ��� ����� ���� ��� ���������� ��� ���� ���� �����\n���������������������������\n������\n�",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_108": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 108 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON: 18.07.2022 \n \nGarbage Dumps \n \n108.  SHRI SRIDHAR KOTAGIRI: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate:  \n \n(a)  whether it is true that there have been increasinginstances of garbage dump turning \ninto mountains in various cities; \n(b)  if so, the steps taken by the Government tocontrol such garbage dumps in major cities \nacross the country; \n(c)  whether the Government made any efforts tocontrol such fires in garbage dumps and \nif so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and \n(d)  whether the Government has released any specialguidelines in this regard and if so, \nthe details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND \nCLIMATE CHANGE  \n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY) \n \n(a) & (b) The Solid Waste Management Rules, 2016, provide the statutory framework for the \nmanagement of solid waste in the country. As per the Rules, the local authorities and village \npanchayats of census towns and urban agglomerations, shall allow only the non-usable, non-\nrecyclable, non-biodegradable, non-combustible and non-reactive inert waste and pre-\nprocessing rejects and residues from waste processing facilities to go to sanitary landfill sites. \nThe rules further stipulate that every effort shall be made to recycle or reuse the rejects to \nachieve the desired objective of zero waste going to landfill. Further, all old open dumpsites \nand existing operational dumpsites are to be investigated and analysed by local authorities \nand village panchayats for their potential of biomining and bio-remediation and wheresover, \nfeasible, take necessary actions to bio-mine or bio-remediate the sites. \n \nThe Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs launched Swachh Bharat Mission – \nUrban (SBM-U) 2.0 in October 2021, with estimated cost of Rs. 1,41,600Crores. The \nobjectives of SBM-U 2.0 include 100 % scientific management of all fractions of waste, its \nsafe disposal in landfills and remediation of all legacy dumpsites and converting them into \ngreen zones. All statutory towns are expected to achieve at least the status of 3-star Garbage \nFree City (GFC) by the end of SBM-U 2.0 on 1st October, 2026. Further, guidelines for \nDisposal of Legacy Waste (Old Municipal Solid Waste) have been issued by Central \nPollution Control Board.Directions under Section 5 of Environment (Protection) Act, \n1986,have also been issued by Central Pollution Control Board to all State Pollution Control \n\nBoards / Pollution Control Committeesfor enforcement of provisions of Solid Waste \nManagement Rules, 2016, regarding biomining of legacy waste. \n \n(c) & (d): The Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs has published Municipal Solid Waste \nManual in 2016. The Manual covers measures for “Fire Prevention and Protection” for the \nuse of urban local bodies, which are responsible for implementing these guidelines. The State \nPollution Control Boards/Pollution Control Committees enforce the provisions of the Solid \nWaste Management Rules, 2016, in their State/Union Territory through local bodies in their \nrespective jurisdictions. \n \n*** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_109": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION AND LITERACY \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 109 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18th JULY, 2022 \n \nProviding Educational Facilities to Backward Communities \n \n109.  SHRI GNANATHIRAVIAM S.: \nSHRI D.M. KATHIR ANAND:  \nSHRI PRATHAP SIMHA: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government is providing funds and any other facilities for the education of \nthe children of Backward Class, Other Backward Class, Scheduled Caste & Scheduled \nTribe communities in Tamil Nadu and Karnataka;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof and the total funds allocated during the last 5 years year-wise;  \n(c)  if not, the reasons for the same; and  \n(d)  the steps taken by the Government to provide adequate support for the educational \ndevelopment of such deprived and o ppressed communities in Tamil Nadu and \nKarnataka? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n(SHRIMATI ANNPURNA DEVI) \n \n(a) to (d) The Right of Children to Free and Compulsory Education (RTE) Act, 2009, \nmandates the appropriate Government to provide free and compulsory elementary education to \nevery child of the age 6 to 14 years in a neighbourhood school. Education is a subject in the  \nconcurrent list of the Constitution and the majority of the schools are under the control of the \nrespective State/UT Governments. Department of School Education and Literacy (DoSEL), \nMinistry of Education is implementing Samagra Shiksha  from 2018 -19 to su pport States and \nUTs, including states of Karnataka and Tamil Nadu  for e nsuring equity and inclusion at all \nlevels of school education . Samagra Shiksha has been aligned with the National Education \nPolicy (NEP) 2020 in order to ensure inclusive and equitabl e, quality and holistic school \neducation. The major objectives of the Scheme are: (i) Support States and UTs in implementing \nthe recommendations of the National Education Policy 2020 (NEP 2020); (ii) Support States in \nimplementation of Right of Children to  Free and Compulsory Education (RTE) Act, 2009; (iii) \nFocus on Early Childhood Care and Education; (iv) Emphasis on Foundational Literacy and \n\nNumeracy; (v) Thrust on Holistic, Integrated, Inclusive and activity based Curriculum and \nPedagogy to impart 21st century skills among the students; (vi) Provision of quality education \nand enhancing learning outcomes of students; (vii) Bridging Social and Gender Gaps in School \nEducation; (viii) Ensuring equity and inclusion at all levels of school education; (ix) \nStrengthening and up -gradation of State Councils for Educational Research and Training \n(SCERTs)/State Institutes of Education and District Institutes for Education and Training \n(DIET) as nodal agency for teacher training; (x) Ensuring safe, secure and conduciv e learning \nenvironment and maintenance of standards in schooling provisions and (xi) Promoting \nvocational education. \n The funds are released to the States and UTs including the states of Karnataka and \nTamil Nadu based on the approved Annual Work Plan & Bud get (AWP&B) of the respective \nState/UT, norms of scheme, General Financial Rules (GFR) and subject to availability of budget \nfor the schemes and physical and financial progress of the State.  The Status of funds released to \nthe State Government of Karnataka  and Tamil Nadu from the year 2018 -19 under Samagra \nShiksha is as follows:- \n(Rs in lakh) \nYear Karnataka Tamil Nadu \n2018-19 62784.00 147444.01 \n2019-20 73032.69 176912.17 \n2020-21 61010.01 162153.74 \n2021-22 47451.63 159882.18 \n \nSamagra Shiksha attempts to reach out to girls, and children belonging to SC, ST, \nMinority communities, etc. SC, ST and minority Special Focus Districts (SFDs) have been \nidentified on the basis of adverse performance on various indicators of enrolment, retention, and \ngender parity, as well as concentration of SC, ST and minority communities. To ensure \nequitable education to the most deprived sections of the society, Samagra Shiksha has various \nprovisions like free textbooks, uniform s, Kasturba Gandhi Balika Vidyalayas for girls, Netaji \nSubhas Chandra Bose Awasiya Vidyalayas/Hostels for areas which are sparsely populated with \nlow density of population (mostly tribal areas).  \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_11": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION No.11 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.07.2022 \n \nDelay in release of Funds to Colleges of Delhi University \n \n*11. SHRI PARVESH SAHIB SINGH VERMA: \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has taken note of the  financial crisis in some colleges of \nUniversity of Delhi  due to delay in release of funds by the Delhi Government  \nand if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) whether the Government has directed to Delhi Government to ensure release of pending \nfunds to colleges of University of Delhi, and if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c) the steps taken by the Government to ensure  financial autonomy of colleges in the \ncountry? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF EDUCATION \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a) to (c): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n***** \n\n11th Position \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (c)  LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION No. 11 FOR 18.07.2022 ASKED BY SHRI PARVESH SAHIB SINGH VERMA, \nHON’BLE MEMBER OF PARLIAMENT REGARDING DELAY IN RELEASE OF FUNDS \nTO COLLEGES OF DELHI UNIVERSITY. \n \n(a) & (b): The University Grants Com mission (UGC) has informed that University of Delhi \nhas 91 Constituent/affiliated Colleges, out of which UGC provides Revenue Grant s to 53 \nDelhi Colleges only. 12 Delhi Colleges are fully funded by the Government of NCT of Delhi \nand the remaining 26 colleges receive funds from other sources.  \nThe UGC has released grants amounting to ₹ 2134.54 cr., ₹ 2446.83 cr. and ₹ 590.51 \ncr. during the F inancial Years 2020 -21, 2021 -22 and 2022 -23 (upto June, 2022) \nrespectively to the 53 Delhi Colleges funded by UGC.  \nA representation was received in the Ministry from all the twelve Principals of DU \nColleges, fully funded by the Government of NCT of Delhi, regarding  the hardships to the \nstaff members and pensioners due to non -release of Grants in Aid. The same was \nforwarded to the Directorate of Higher Education, Government of NCT of Delhi for taking \nnecessary action in the matter. The UGC has further informed that t he University of Delhi \nhas also communicated that it has received representations from staff of 12 colleges \nregarding delay in release of funds by the Government of NCT of Delhi . The University of \nDelhi has written to the Directorate of Higher Education, Government of NCT of Delhi  for \ntimely release of grants to the Colleges for salary and other expenses.  \nThe University of Delhi has informed that the Government of NCT of Delhi has also \nbeen releasing grants to the 12 Colleges fully funded by them, albeit delayed. \n \n(c): The University Grants Commission (Conferment of Autonomous Status Upon Colleges \nand Measures for Maintenance of Standards in Autonomous Colleges) Regulations, 2018 \nhave been notified on 12th February, 2018 on the guiding principle that college autonomy is \ninstrumental for promoting broad based quality education and excellence . Under the \nprovisions of these Regulations the Autonomous C olleges can fix fees of the courses at \ntheir own level and have also been exempted from paying  affiliation fee to the parent \nUniversity every year. \n*****\n\n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_110": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.110 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.07.2022 \n \nPROMOTION OF INDIAN HERITAGE IN RAJASTHAN \n \n110. SHRI DEVJI M. PATEL: \nWill the Minister of Culture be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government proposes to promote India’s heritage by identifying potential \nhistorical and archaeological marvels of the country particularly at Jalore- Sirohi in \nRajasthan; \n  (b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) whether the Government propose s to protect these sites by developing them into a \nmuseum or historical tourist spots? \n \nANSWER  \n \n                                      THE MINISTER OF CULTURE, TOURISM AND \n                                      DEVELOPMENT OF NORTH EASTERN REGION \n \n                                                      (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n (a)  \n  & \n (b) \nGovernment of India protects monuments and sites declared as of National \nImportance. There are 3,693 such monuments and sites including 163 located in the \nState of Rajasthan. However, there is no centrally protected monument in Jalore and \nSirohi District. \n (c) Monuments and sites of National importance are places of tourist attraction . \nProtection, preservation and conservation of such monuments and sites is  a regular \nphenomenon taken up by Archaeological Survey of India. To extend better \nexperience to tourists , amenities like drinking water, toilets, pathways, ramps, etc. \nbesides facilities like cafeteria, interpretation centre, informative signages, Braille  \nsignages, benches, wheel chairs, illumination, sound and light shows, etc. are \nprovided based on requirement and availability of resources. \nFurther, Archaeological Survey of India maintains 52 archaeological site museums. \n \n*****  ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_111": "�\n������������������� �\n��������������������������������� �\n��������� �\n����������������������� ��� �\n���������������������������� �\n�\n������������������������������������������������������ �\n�\n���������������������������������������������������\n�\n�� ����� ���� ��������� ��� ������� ���� ��������� �� ��� �������� ���\n��������\n�\n����������� ���� ���������� ��� ���������� ������ ���������� ���� ����������\n�������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������� ��� ������ ���� �������� ���� ������� ��� �������� ��������\n���� ���������� ����� ������ ���� ������� � ������ ��� �� ��������� ���\n��������������������������������������������������� �\n����������������������������������������������������������������������\n�� ��������������� ������������� ���� �������������� ���������� ����\n�������������� �\n����������� ���� ������ ��� �������� � �������� ������� ������������ ����������\n�������� ���������� �������� �������� �������� ������ ������ ����\n�������������������������������������� �\n��������������������������������������������������������������� �\n�\n������ �\n�\n����������������� �������������������������� �\n����� �������������� ��\n�\n����� � ������� ���� ���������� ����� ���� ��������� �������� ���������������������� ������������\n���������� ������ ���������� ��������� ���� ��� ������ ����������� ���\n��������� ���� ����������� ����� ��� �������� �������� �������� ��������\n����������� ������ ����������� ��� ����� ���� ���� ���������� ����� ���\n������� �\n���� ������� ��� ����������� ������ ��� �������� ��������� ����\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������� �������������������� �\n��������� ��\n\n��������\n���������������� � ���������� � ������� ������ �\n�������������� � � � � � � �\n�������������� � �� � � � � �\n�\n����� � ��� ��������� ���� ���������� �������� ��� ������� �������������\n��������� ���������� ���� ��������� ������ � ��������� ��������������\n����������������������������������������������� �\n����������� ���� �������� ��� ������� ������ ��� ������ ������� ���� ������� ���\n��������� �\n�\n����������������������������������������������������������������������\n� �\n\n�\n�������� �\n�������� ���������� ��� ��� ������ ��� ����� ���� �� ���� ��� ���� ������\n���������� ��������� ���� ���� ���� ������� ��� ����������� ������ ��� �����\n������� ������� ���������� ��������� ��� �������� ���� ������ ��� ����\n����������������� �\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������\n�\n��������� ������������������� ��������������������� �������������� ��������������\n�\n��� ���������������� ��� ��� ���\n��� ��������������� ��� ��� ���\n��� ������������������\n��������\n�� ��� ���\n��� ����������������� �� ��� ���\n��� ������������ ��� ��� ���\n��� ����������� ��� ��� ���\n�\n����� �",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_112": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 112 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.07.2022 \n \nSOCIAL SECURITY CONCERNS OF INDIANS WORKING ABROAD \n \n112. SHRI MANICKAM TAGORE B.:  \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)whether it is a fact that the Government has taken up the social \nsecurity concerns of Indians working abroad at the International \nLabour Conference of the International Labour Organisation;  \n(b)if so, the details thereof;  \n(c)whether it is also true that India has signed social security agreements \nwith many countries or provinces and with one already operational;  \n(d)whether it is a fact that India has emphasised that migration should \nnot be considered as trafficking and that the s ocial security of Indians \nworking abroad should be ensured; and \n(e)if so, the response received by the Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) to (e): Social Security concerns of Indians working abroad are \ngoverned by various conventions of International Labour Organization  \n(ILO) and is operationalized through signing of social security \nagreements. The Social Security agreements protect the rights of \nInternational workers in terms of detac hment, totalization and portability \nbenefits. \n \nIndia has signed social security agreements with 19 countries \nand 1 province  (Quebec). Out of the total 20 a greements signed 19 have \nentered into force. \n \n   For fostering regular migration and to encourage the  mobility \nof the workforce, India has been entering into Migration and Mobility \nAgreements with different countries. The agreement, inter -alia contains \nthe provision regarding cooperation between two sides in fight against \nirregular migration and trafficking in human beings. \n******* ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_113": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT & ENTERPRENEURSHIP \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 113 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.07.2022 \n \nCOURSES TO NEXT GENERATION \n \n113. SHRI PATEL HASMUKHBHAI SOMABHAI:  \n \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT & ENTERPRENE URSHIP be \npleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the public or private companies have c ollaborated with the Ministry \nfor launching Courses to make the next generation s killed enough to take up future \njobs; and \n(b)  if so, the details thereof along with the step s taken by the Government in this \nregard?  \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF  \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT & ENTERPRENEURSHIP \n(SHRI RAJEEV CHANDRASEKHAR) \n \n(a) Yes, Sir.  \n \n(b) (I) The Directorate General of Training (DGT), Ministry of Skills Development \nand Entrepreneurship has collaborated with various tech companies like IBM, Cisco, \nMicrosoft, Quest Alliance, etc. for enhancing the t raining activities to make the next \ngeneration skilled enough to take up future jobs. D igital learning content and links to \nvarious other new-age programs offered by the tech companies has been provided \non the Bharatskills portal. (https://bharatskills@gov.in  ) \n \n  Details of various initiatives undertaken are as follows: \ni. A two-year Advanced Diploma (Vocational) in IT, Net working and Cloud \nComputing (ADIT) - NSQF Level-6 has been launched i n collaboration with IBM \n(under CSR), in the National Skills Training Instit ute (NSTIs) under DGT, across the \ncountry. IBM SkillsBuild, a Digital Learning Platform for fu ture skills has also been linked \nthrough Bharatskills portal. \n \nii. Modern employability skills’ labs have been set -up by Quest Alliance in \nwomen NSTIs and is continuously supporting in train ing of trainers and trainees and \nupgradation of curriculum. \n \niii. Cisco has established Cisco Certified Network Associate (CCNA) Labs at \nsome NSTIs and is providing training for CCNA certi fications to the trainers & \ntrainees.  \n \niv. Digital blended content for Computer Operator a nd Programming Assistant \n(COPA) trade of Craftsmen Training Scheme (CTS) has  been developed by \nMicrosoft. The Microsoft Technology Associate (MTA)  course and certification for ITI \nCOPA students has also been provided by Microsoft. \n\nAbout 14.0 Lakh trainees have benefitted through th e learning pathways provided by \nthese industries: \n \nS. No.  Tech companies  Trainees T rained  \n 1 IBM India Pvt. Ltd. 10,17,320 \n2 Cisco Systems India Pvt. Ltd 11,666 \n3 Quest Alliance 3,12,532 \n4 Microsoft – NASSCOM Foundation 39,222 \n5 SAP India 1,110 \n6 Adobe India 24.360 \n                                                             Total  14,06,210  \n \n(II) The National Skill Development Corporation (NSDC) has d eveloped an \neLearning portal —eSkill India ( https://eskillindia.org  ) which collaborates with Tech \ncompanies in digital skilling and Edtech domain for  offering a bouquet of online \ncourses (both self-paced and hybrid) to the Indian youth. The eskill india best-in-\nclass digital partnerships include Microsoft, IBM, California State University -Long \nBeach, Saylor Academy (US),CISCO, Khan Academy, TCS , Simplilearn, PMA \nIndia and many more are collectively offering over 1600 cataloged courses. These \nare catering to Industry 4.0 skills including AI, B ig data, ML, Cybersecurity, Robotic \nProcess Automation (RPA), IoT, Project management, Digital Marketing and many \nmore. These partnerships demonstrate the enablement  of digital opportunity for \nskilling in participation with the private sector. \nAdditionally, online learning opportunities related  to Employability skills, language \nskills and Entrepreneurship, such as Digital Litera cy, Communication skills, Business \nEtiquette, entrepreneurial mindset, customer centri city, time management and \nprofessional 2ehavior at workplace is also being of fered through multiple digital \npartnerships with Industry members on the portal. T hese courses would help \nparticipants enhance their employability skills alo ng with domain skills. The courses \nbeing offered on eSkill India help participants learn English language skills along with \ncommunication and basic computer skills, which are must-have skills in the current \ntimes. \nThese self-paced courses can be accessed anytime an ywhere by Indian Youth. \nThese courses may be offered under self-paced mode and blended learning \nmode.The rich offerings have resulted in exceeding 2 million enrolments. Majority of \ne-course on eSkill India are offered for free. \nNSDC has also partnered with multiple public and pr ivate entities and has launched \nvarious courses for skilling of the candidates in a spirational and future jobs.List of \nsome of the entities with whom NSDC partners and th e program offered is at \nAnnexure.  \n \n  \n\n Annexure  \nAnnexure referred to in replyto Lok Sabha USQ 113 t o be answered on \n18.7.2022 regarding NSDC’s partnership with differe nt entities offering \nfuture and next generation courses \nS. No.  Name of the Partner  Courses  Offered  \n1 Imarticus Learning \nPrivate Limited \n·       Big Data and Machine Learning \n·       Data Science Pro Degree \n·       Fintech Pro Degree \n·       Post Graduate in Data Analytics \n·       Post Graduate Program in Analytics &Artificial \nIntelligence (PGAA) \n2. Appworx IT Solutions \nPrivate Limited \n·       Graphic Designing \n·       Digital and Online Marketing \n·       Artificial Intelligence \n·       PHP Magneto \n3. Centre for Sustainable \nDevelopment \n·       Internet of Things (IoT) \n·       Data Science and Analytics \n·       Solid Waste Management \n·       Climate Resilient Cities and Disaster Management \n·       Green Entrepreneurship Program \n4. UpGrad \n·       PG Certificate in Blockchain Technology \n·       PG Certificate in Data Science \n·       PG Certificate in ML & DL \n5. Image Creative ·       Certificate in 2D animation \n·       C-Tech Higher Diploma in 3D and Visual effects \n6. CADD \n·       AutoCAD 2D \n·       VFX Editor \n·       Certificate course on Jewelry making \n7. Simulanis Solutions \nPrivate Limited \n·       Fire safety Simulator Training \n·       Paint Simulator Training \n8. \nTransorze Information \nProcessing Solutions \nPrivate Limited \n·       Medical Scribing training \n·       Advance Digital marketing \n·       Medical Transcription Training \n·       Occupational English Test (OET) \n9. \nSunstone Education \nTechnology Private \nLimited \n·       Specialization course on Banking Financial Service \n& Insurance \n·       Business Process Management (Finance & \nAccounting) \n·       Specialized course on HR Executive – Payroll and \nEmployee Data Management \n·       Specialized Course on Digital Marketing \n·       Specialization course on Business Enhancer /Multi \nchannel Retailer \n10. H.K. Kalchuri Education \nTrust \n·       Electric vehicle service lead technician \n·       Electric vehicle quality control inspector \n·       AI – Database administrator \n·       User Experience Designer \n11. \nTalent Invigoration and \nSports Management \nPrivate Limited \n·       Sports Coach (Residential) \n·       Sports Coach (New Coach and Refresher \nworkshops) \n·       Sports Coach (University tie-ups non-residential \n\nprogram) \n12. Drone Destination \n·       Drone Pilot / Operations \n·       Training for maintenance and repair of Drone \n·       Drone training for Agriculture \n13. Clear Sky Private Limited  \n·       Drone Pilot / Operations \n·       Training for maintenance and repair of Drone \n·       Drone training for Agriculture \n14. Infovalley Private Limited \n·       Diploma in Aviation Management \n·       Airline first officer \n·       Diploma in Hospitality and Tourism Management \n15. Frostees India Private \nLimited \n·       Auto Service Technician \n·       Field Technician – Air Conditioner \n·       Field Technician Refrigerator \n16. Masai School, (Nolan \nEdutech Pvt Ltd) \n·       Full Stack Web Development \n·       Full Stack Android Development \n·       Full Stack UI UX Design \n·       Data Analytics \n \n17. \nFunction Up, (Fanplay \nGames and Media Pvt. \nLtd) \n·       Backend Engineering \n·       Frontend Engineering \n·       Data Science \n·       Digital Marketing \n·       Web 3.0 \n18. \nSkill Lync (EpowerX \nLearning Technologies \nPvt Ltd) \n·       Masters Certification Program in Hybrid EV Design \n·       Design Engineer \n·       Masters Certification in High Rise building design \n·       CFD Engineer Master’s Certification Program \n·       Data Analytics & Data Science \n·       Embedded Systems for EV Applications \n·       Full Stack Web Development \n19. Skillinabox \nD2D Vocational Skilling platform helping learners get \nskilled right at their doorstep and kick start their \ncareers. Fashion designerMakeup artist \n20. LetsEndorse \nDevelopment Pvt Ltd \nEnd to end enabling Programs for micro/ nano-\nentrepreneurs \n21. Nxtwave Disruptive \nTechnologies Pvt Ltd \nIntensive Programs in IT / Software development in \nVernacular languages \n  \n22. Law Sikho \n                  \n·       Upskilling courses for lawyers \n·    Courses for non-lawyers – Remote jobs (like Virtual \nAssistant, Para Legal) \n23. Mythiksha School Grey Collar Jobs- Business Development, Marketing, \nCustomer Service, Business & Corporate finance \n24. Skyll.Me Personal Banker, Retail BFSI, BFSI Sales \n25. Multibhashi Language Training – Japanese, German, English \n  \n  \n******* ",
