��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������������������������������������\n����",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_125": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF PANCHAYATI RAJ \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *125 \nANSWERED ON 26.07.2022 \n  \nFUNDS UNDER BRGF AND RGSY \n \n*125   SHRI VINAYAK RAUT: \nSHRI THOMAS CHAZHIKADAN: \n \nWill the Minister of PANCHAYATI RAJ be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has granted any funds under the Backward Regions Grant \nFund (BRGF) to any districts in the country especially in the State of Kerala; \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the beneficiaries of the schemes run with the help of these \nfunds; \n(c) whether it is a fact that funds would be provided under Rashtriya Gram Swaraj Yojana \n(RGSY) to districts uncovered under BRGF; \n(d) the details of districts that are not covered under BRGF in the States of Andhra Pradesh \nand Maharashtra; and  \n(e) the details of total funds provided to these districts under RGSY during each of the last \nfive years and the current year? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF PANCHAYATI RAJ \n \n(SHRI GIRIRAJ SINGH)  \n  \n(a) to (e) A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (e ) of LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *125 FOR ANSWER ON 26.07.2022 REGARDING FUNDS \nUNDER BRGF AND RGSY. \n \n(a)& (b): The Ministry of Panchayati Raj (MoPR) was implementing the Backward Regions Grant \nFund (BRGF) Programme (District component) during the period 2006 -07 to 2014 -15 in 272 \ndistricts of 28 States of the country, including in the State of Kerala. During this period, grants of \nRs. 26,622.31 crore was released for the districts covered under the scheme.  Two districts of \nKerala, namely Palakkad and Wayanad , were covered under the scheme to which grants of \nRs.101.47 crore and Rs.55.85 crore, respectively, was released during the said period. The prime \nobjective of the Programme was to bridge the critical gaps in local infrastructure and other \ndevelopment requirements which were not being adequately met through the existing financial \ninflows during that time and as such the Programme was not beneficiary oriented.  \n \n(c):  No, sir as the scheme of Rashtriya Gram Swaraj  Yojana (RGSY) is not under \nimplementation at present. The scheme was subsumed into Rajiv Gandhi Panchayat Sashaktikaran \nAbhiyan (RGPSA) scheme implemented from the year 2013, which has further been restructured \nas Rashtriya Gram Swaraj Abhiyan (RGSA) since 2018 -19. RGSA scheme is implemented \nthroughout the country for strengthening Panchayati Raj Institutions in all the Districts.    \n \n(d): The districts in the States of Andhra Pradesh and Maharashtra which were not covered \nunder the BRGF Programme are at Annexure. \n \n(e):  Since the scheme of RGSY is not under implementation, the question does not arise. \n \n***  \n\nAnnexure \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (d) of Lok Sabha Starred Question No. *125 answered \non 26.07.2022. \n \nDistricts not covered under Backward Region Grant Fund Programme in the States of \nAndhra Pradesh and Maharashtra \n \nSl.No. State Sl.No. Districts not covered under BRGF Programme \n1 Andhra \nPradesh \n1.  Alluri Sitharama Raju \n2.  Anakapalli \n3.  Annamayya \n4.  Bapatla \n5.  East Godavari \n6.  Eluru \n7.  Guntur \n8.  Kakinada \n9.  Konaseema \n10.  Krishna \n11.  Kurnool \n12.  Nandyal \n13.  NTR \n14.  Palnadu \n15.  Parvathipuram Manyam \n16.  Prakasam \n17.  SPSR Nellore \n18.  Srikakulam \n19.  Sri Sathya Sai \n20.  Tirupati \n21.  Visakhapatanam \n22.  West Godavari \n2 Maharashtra 1.  Akola \n2.  Beed \n3.  Buldhana \n4.  Jalgaon \n5.  Jalna \n6.  Kolhapur \n7.  Latur \n8.  Mumbai \n9.  Mumbai Suburban \n10.  Nagpur \n11.  Nashik \n12.  Osmanabad \n13.  Palghar \n\nSl.No. State Sl.No. Districts not covered under BRGF Programme \n14.  Parbhani \n15.  Pune \n16.  Raigad \n17.  Ratnagiri \n18.  Sangli \n19.  Satara \n20.  Sindhudurg \n21.  Solapur \n22.  Thane \n23.  Wardha \n24.  Washim \nNote: Data of districts as per Local Government Directory (https://lgdirectory.gov.in/) \n \n \n *** ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_126": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 126 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.07.2022 \n \nEXCAVATIONS AT KEEZHADI \n126. SHRI SHANMUGA SUNDARAM K.: \n \nWill the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether ASI is aware of the excavation of menhirs in Pollachi Lok Sabha constituency \nin Tamil Nadu and the details thereof; \n \n(b) the amount of funds allocated, disbursed and utilised for the same; \n \n(c) whether the Government has taken steps to conduct further excavations at Keezhadi, \nAdichanallur and Pollachi and if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(d) if  not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF CULTURE, TOURISM AND DEVELOPMENT OF NORTH EASTERN \nREGION \n \n(SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n \n(a) \n& \n(b) \nChennai Circle of Archaeological Survey of India (ASI) had carried out exploration in \n1963 where the presence of Menhirs was reported in Pollachi. \nSince no excavation was carried out, the question of funds allocated does not arise. \n \n(c) \n& \n(d) \nThe excavations at Keezhadi and Adichanallur are being conducted by State \nDepartment of Archaeology, Tamil Nadu and Trichy Circle, ASI respectively.  \n \nNo proposal has been received for excavation at Pollachi. \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_127": " \n  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK SABHA \n STARRED QUESTION NO. 127 \n  ANSWERED ON 26/07/2022 \n \nBENEFICIARIES UNDER PMAY-G \n \n*127.  SHRI VIJAYAKUMAR (ALIAS) VIJAY VASANTH: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the total number of applications received and the  eligible beneficiaries under Pradhan \nMantri Awas Yojana - Gramin (PMAY-G) during each of the last three years, State-wise; \n(b)  the targets fixed and achievements made under the yojana till date, State-wise; \n(c)  whether any steps are being taken by the  Government to ensure 100 percent housing for \nall families in the rural areas of the country; and \n(d)  if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SHRI GIRIRAJ SINGH) \n \n(a) to (d):        A statement is laid on the table of the House.   \n   \n  \n   \n    \n  \n\n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PART (a) to (d) OF LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION \nNO. 127 TO BE ANSWERED ON 26.07.2022 REGARDING BENEFICIARIES UNDER \nPMAY-G \n  \n(a)   Under PMAY -G, the target is the construction of 2.95 crore houses to achieve the \nobjective of “Housing for All” in rural areas. Under PMAY -G, the beneficiaries have been \nidentified based on the housing deprivation paramet ers prescribed under Socio Economic Caste \nCensus (SECC) 2011. After due verification by the Gram Sabha and completion of Appellate \nProcess, Gram panchayat -wise Permanent Wait List (PWL) is prepared. The auto generated \npriority list of households from SECC,  2011 database was provided to the States/Union \nTerritories (UTs) for conducting Gram Sabha meetings to finalize the PWL. As on 20.07.2022, a \ntotal of 2.15 crore households have been identified and included in PWL from SECC, 2011. \nFurther, the details of s uch households who   claimed to be left out from the SECC 2011 based \nPWL  and eligible for inclusion in PWL, have been captured in the Awaas+ Survey, 2018. The \nSurvey was conducted during January, 2018 and 7th March, 2019. In this exercise, the States/UTs \nhave uploaded details of additional households. In order to fill the gap of 80 lakh houses (2.95C -\n2.15), Awaas+ data is being utilized. Out of this, a target of 63.68 lakh has been allocated to the \nStates/UTs till date.   \nNo application from households is r equired under PMAY -G as beneficiaries are identified from \nthe PWL of PMAY-G based on SECC 2011 and finalised Awaas+ list. \n(b)   The State-wise details of target fixed and achievement made under the scheme till date is \ngiven in Annexure. \n(c) & (d) The Minis try has taken the following initiatives to ensure timely completion of the \nhouses under PMAY-G:  \n   \ni. Regular review of progress at the level of Ministry.   \nii. Launch of PMAY-G Dashboard for monitoring and supervision of the scheme.  \niii. Timely allocation of targets to the States/UTs and release of adequate funds.  \niv. Regular follow ups with the State to ensure release of central and state share of funds and \nprovision of land to landless beneficiaries in rural areas.  \nv. Awards to the best performing States/UTs, Districts  based on performance index \ndashboard, thereby creating healthy competition and motivation among the States/UTs \nfor achieving the set targets. \n***** \n  \n\nAnnexure \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PART (b) OF LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 127 TO BE \nANSWERED ON 26.07.2022 REGARDING BENEFICIARIES UNDER PMAY-G \n  \nState/UT-wise details of target fixed and achievements (as on 20.07.2022) under PMAY-G. \n  \n(units in no.) \nSNo State/UT \nTarget allocated  to \nStates/UTs  \nby MoRD \nTotal houses  \nconstructed  \n  \n  \n1 Arunachal Pradesh 41,596                     5,139    \n2 Assam 19,13,026               5,88,326    \n3 Bihar 38,83,611            26,09,969    \n4 Chhattisgarh 10,97,150               8,25,282    \n5 Goa 1,707                        133    \n6 Gujarat 4,49,167               3,81,977    \n7 Haryana 30,789                  21,014    \n8 Himachal Pradesh 15,483                  10,200    \n9 Jammu and Kashmir 2,01,633                  91,953    \n10 Jharkhand 16,03,268            12,26,387    \n11 Kerala 42,212                  20,311    \n12 Madhya Pradesh 37,89,400            26,05,232    \n13 Maharashtra 15,05,983               8,31,859    \n14 Manipur 46,166                  15,035    \n15 Meghalaya 81,677                  30,316    \n16 Mizoram 20,518                     5,536    \n17 Nagaland 24,775                     4,239    \n18 Odisha 26,95,837            16,95,941    \n19 Punjab 41,117                  23,170    \n20 Rajasthan 17,33,959            13,18,243    \n21 Sikkim 1,409                     1,070    \n22 Tamil Nadu 8,17,439               3,70,658    \n23 Tripura 2,82,238               1,10,400    \n24 Uttar Pradesh 26,15,951            25,75,399    \n25 Uttarakhand 29,052                  22,341    \n26 West Bengal 34,82,359            33,40,432    \n27 Andaman and Nicobar 1,337                     1,117    \n28 \nDadra and Nagar Haveli \n& Daman and Diu 6,831                 2,160    \n29 Lakshadweep 45                           \n45    \n30 Puducherry* -                            -\n      \n31 Andhra Pradesh 2,56,270                  46,707    \n32 Karnataka 3,07,746               1,01,630    \n33 Telangana* -                            -\n      \n34 Ladakh 1,992                     1,428    \n  Total 2,70,21,743         1,88,83,649    \n * Puducherry and Telengana are not implementing PMAY-G.  \n*** ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_128": " \n  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK SABHA \n STARRED QUESTION NO. 128 \n  ANSWERED ON 26/07/2022 \n \nRENAMING OF SCHEMES \n \n*128.  SHRI NAKUL KAMAL NATH: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the details of the schemes of the Ministry which have been renamed by the Government \nafter 2014; and \n(b)  the details of the funds allocated to these schemes after 2014? \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SHRI GIRIRAJ SINGH) \n \n(a)  &  (b):  A Statement is laid on the table of the House. \n  \n  \n  \n\n \n \nStatement referred to in reply to parts (a) & (b) of the Lok Sabha Starred Question \nNo. 128 for reply on 26.07.2022 \n  \n(a) The details of the schemes of the Ministry of Rural Development which have been \nrenamed by the Government after 2014 are as under: \ni. The erstwhile Rural Housing Scheme Indira Awas Yojana (IAY) has been \nrestructured into Pradhan Mantri Awaas Yojana -Gramin (PMAY -G) with effect \nfrom 1st April, 2016. To achieve the objective of  “Housing For All”, a total target \nof 2.95 crore houses is set under PMAY -G. PMAY-G aims to provide financial \nassistance to all Rural House -less Households and Households living in kutcha \nand dilapidated houses.  \nii. The Centrally Sponsored Scheme National  Rural Livelihoods Mission (NRLM) \nwas renamed as Deendayal Antyodaya Yojana - National Rural Livelihoods \nMission (DAY-NRLM) in 2015. Its skill Component has been renamed as Deen \nDayal Upadhyaya -Grameen Kaushalya Yojana (DDUGKY) in 2014. DAY-\nNRLM aims at mobilizing women from about 10 crore rural poor households into \nSelf Help Groups (SHGs) and provide them long -term support such that they \ndiversify their livelihoods, improve their incomes and quality of life.  \n(b) The year-wise details of Budget Estimate and Revised Estimate from 2015-16 to 2022-23 \nis at Annexure. \n ****** \n  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n  \n\n \nAnnexure \nAnnexure referred in reply to part (b) of  Lok Sabha Starred Question No. 128 for \nreply          on 26.7.2022 \n  \n            (i) The year-wise details of Budget Estimate and Revised Estimate under PMAY-G: \n                                                                                                                                      (Rs. in crore) \nFinancial \nYear \nBudget Estimate (BE) Revised  Estimate (RE) \n2015-16 10025.00 10025.00 \n2016-17 15000.00 16078.60 \n2017-18 23000.00 22832.31 \n2018-19 21000.00 19600.00 \n2019-20 19000.00 18455.20 \n2020-21 19500.00 19500.00 \n2021-22 30057.15 30056.87 \n2022-23 20000.00 - \n  \n          (ii) Budget Allocations Under DAY-NRLM since 2014-15 are as under: \n                                             \n                                                                                      (Rs. in crore) \n  \n  \n  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n              * including Startup Village Entrepreneurship Programme (SVEP) \n \n \n                                 **** \n \nFinancial  \nYear Budget Estimate (BE) Revised  Estimate (RE) \n2015-16* 2705 2705 \n2016-17 3000 3168.15 \n2017-18 4500 4350.83 \n2018-19 5750 5783.5 \n2019-20 9024 9024 \n2020-21 9210.04 9209.53 \n2021-22 13677.61 10813.89 \n2022-23 13336.42  - ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_129": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENTOF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *129 \nANSWERED ON 26TH JULY, 2022 \n \n \nINADEQUATE FOOD PROCESSING FACILITIES \n  \n \n*129. SHRI K. NAVASKANI: \n           \n       Will the Minister of FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES be pleased to state: \n \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware that though India is the world’s second largest producer of \nfruits and vegetables, a sizeable part of the same goes waste in the absence of adequate \nprocessing facilities; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof along with the corrective steps taken/being taken by the Government \nto achieve the aim of raising the processing level to 25 percent by 2025; and \n \n(c) the details of the schemes rolled out for food processing along with the funds allocated for the \nsame and the progress made therein especially in Tamil Nadu? \n \nANSWER \n \n \nTHE MINISTER OF FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \n(SHRI PASHUPATI KUMAR PARAS) \n \n(a) to (c): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n***** \n  \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO (c) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO.*129 FOR ANSWER ON 26TH JULY,2022 ON “INADEQUAE FOOD \nPPROCESSING FACILITIES” \n \n(a) to (c):  Yes, Sir. India is the second largest producer of fruits &  vegetables in the world and the \nestimated annual harvest and post -harvest losses in these sub -sector varies from 4.58% to 15.88%.  \nWith a view to raise the processing level, Ministry of Food Processing Industries  (MoFPI) is \nimplementing a central sector umbrella sche me known as “Pradhan Mantri Kisan Sampada Yojana \n(PMKSY)” since 2016. Apart from PMKSY, as a part of Aatmanirbhar  Bharat Abhiyan, the Ministry \nis also implementing a centrally sponsored Prime Ministers’ Formalization of Micro food proces sing \nEnterprises (PMFME) Scheme since 2020 and a central sector Product Linked Incentive (PLI) Scheme \nsince 2021. \n \nUnder PMKSY, 1028 projects have been sanctioned  across the country , out of which 630 \nprojects have been completed. In Tamil Nadu, a total 81 number of projects have been sanctioned and \n48 projects have been completed under various component schemes of PMKSY. \n \nUnder PMFME scheme, out of 4,869 loan applications sanction ed in the country, 445 loan \napplication has been sanctioned for Tamil Nadu. Under PLI Scheme, out of 56 applicants with proposed \ninvestment to the tune of Rs 7721.7 Crore approved in the country, 10 applicants with proposed \ninvestment to the tune of Rs 676.9 Crore has been approved for Tamil Nadu. \n \nThe details of funds allocated and utilized under various schemes in 2021 -22 is given at \nAnnexure. \n \n**** \n \n \n \n \n \n  \n\nANNEXURE \n \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO (c) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. *129 FOR ANSWER ON 2 6TH JULY, 2022 REGARDING “ INADEQUATE \nFOOD PROCESSING FACILITIES”.  \n \nThe fund allocated under PM Kisan Sampada Yojana (PM KSY) in FY 2021-22 \n (Rs. in Crore) \nS.No. Name of the Scheme Budget \nEstimates \nRevised \nEstimates \nActual \nExpenditure \n1 Scheme for Mega Food Parks 47.60 54.37 52.59 \n2 Scheme of Infrastructure for Agro Processing \nClusters \n37.50 53.90 49.08 \n3 Scheme for Integrated Cold Chain and Value \nAddition Infrastructure \n227.60 263.00 225.31 \n4 Scheme for Creation/ Expansion of Food \nProcessing and Preservation Capacities \n162.00 242.50 238.08 \n5 Scheme for Creation of  Backward and Forward \nLinkages \n51.70 40.79 32.98 \n6 Scheme of Food Safety and Quality Assurance \nInfrastructure \n45.20 46.70 34.25 \n7 Scheme for Operation Greens 73.40 74.50 68.15 \n \nThe fund allocated under PM Formalization of Micro Food Processing Enterprises (PMFME) \nin FY 2021-22 \n (Rs. in Crore) \nS.No. Name of the Scheme Budget \nEstimates \nRevised \nEstimates \nActual \nExpenditure \n1 PM Formalization of Micro Food Processing  \nEnterprises \n500 399 326.46 \n \nThe fund allocated under P roduction linked Incentive Scheme for Food Processing Industries \n(PLIS) in FY 2021-22 \n (Rs. in Crore) \nS.No. Name of the Scheme Budget \nEstimates \nRevised \nEstimates \nActual \nExpenditure \n1 Production Linked Incentive Scheme 10 10 9.27 \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_13": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.†*13 \nANSWERED ON 18.07.2022 \n \nPROJECTS UNDER PRASAD SCHEME IN MAHARASHTRA \n \n†*13.SHRI VINAYAK RAUT:  \nSHRI KRUPAL BALAJI TUMANE:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  the details of the programmes selected under the Pilgrimage \nRejuvenation and Spiritual Augmentation Drive (PRASAD) \nScheme in Maharashtra;  \n(b)  the number of projects completed during the last three years and \nthe details of funds released so far;  \n(c)  whether any project has been taken up for development in \nRatnagiri-Sindhdurg and Ramtek -Nagpur district and if so, the \ndetails thereof;  \n(d)  the number of projects to be taken up under PRASAD scheme; \nand  \n(e)  the speedy action taken by the Government to com plete the \nabove projects at the earliest and the time by which these \nprojects are likely to be completed? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM         (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n(a) to (e): A statement is laid on the table of the House. \n*********** \n\nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. †*13 ANSWERED ON 18.07.2022  REGARDING \nPROJECTS UNDER PRASAD SCHEME IN MAHARASHTRA. \n \n \n(a) & (b): As per the proposal received from the State Government, \none project viz. Development of Trimbakeshwar,  Nasik has been \napproved at the cost of Rs. 37.81 Cr . in Jan. 2018 under PRASHAD \nScheme of the Ministry of Tourism for the State of Maharashtra.  The \nproject has been revised in Feb. 2022 with the cost Rs.52.92  Cr. Till \ndate an amount of Rs.24.23 Cr. has been released for the project. \n \n(c): No project has been approved under PRASHAD Scheme for \ndevelopment in Rathnagiri -Sindhdurg and Ramtek -Nagpur district.  \nHowever, one project viz. Development of Sindhudurg Coastal Circuit \n(Shiroda Beach, Sagareshwar, Tark arli, Vijaydurg (Beach & Creek), \nDevgad (Fort & Beach), Mitbhav, Tondavali,  Mochemad and Nivati \nFort) has been approved under Swadesh Darshan Scheme of the \nMinistry in the year 2016.  Present revise d cost of the project is Rs. \n19.06 Cr.  The physical exec ution of the approved components in the \nproject is complete.  \n \n(d): Selection of new destination/ places and approval of projects for \ndevelopment under PRASHAD Scheme of Ministry of Tourism is a \ncontinuous process, which is done on receipt of suitable proposals \nfrom the respective State Governments/Union Territories \nAdministrations in line with the Scheme Guidelines.  \n \n(e): An internal dashboard has been developed for real time \nmonitoring of execution of the projects.  Periodically reviews on the \n\nprogress of  the execution of the projects are also undertaken and \nnecessary directions/advices, as required are rendered to the State \nGovernments/Union Territory Administrations concerned.  Site visits by \nteams from the Ministry are also conducted to verify the repor ted \nphysical and financial progress by the concerned executing agencies.  \nIn case of need, Ministry also supports  the State/ UTs in obtaining \nrequired statutory clearances from the concerned agencies.   \n******** ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_130": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 130 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.07.2022 \n \nSea Erosion \n \n130. SHRI K. NAVASKANI \n \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) Whether the Government has taken any measures to curtail the sea erosion and  \nany advanced plan to address this; and \n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE \nCHANGE  \n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY) \n \n \n(a) & (b) The Government is committed for taking proactive steps in combating sea erosion \nand protection of India’s coastal areas and coastal communities. Some of the measures \ntaken are as follows: \n \ni. The Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change (MoEFCC) has notified \nCoastal Regulation Zone Notification, 2019 with a view to conserve and protect \ncoastal stretches, marine areas and to ensure livelihood security to the fisherand other \nlocal communities. As per CRZ notification, 2019, certain coastal areas were declared \nas coastal regulation zone, wherein setting up of industries and expansion of \nindustries are prohibited activities and other developmental activities/projects are \nregulated/ permitted as per provisions of the said notification. The notification also \nprovides for ‘No Development Zones’ (NDZ) along various categories of coastal \nareas to protect India’s coastline from encroachment, erosion and accretion. \nii. MoEFCC has delineated the hazard line for the entire coast of the country. The \nhazard line is indicative of the shoreline changes, including sea level rise due to \nclimate change. This line is to be used by agencies in Coastal States as a tool for \nDisaster Management including planning of adaptive and mitigation measures. The \nhazard line features in the new Coastal Zone Management Plans of the coastal States / \nUnion territories approved by the MoEFCC.  \n\niii. A national strategy for coastal protection along with guidelines has been framed for \nall Coastal States and Union Territories by MoEFCC. \niv. The Flood Management scheme of Ministry of Jal Shakti, including anti-sea erosion \nschemes, are planned and executed by the State Governments with their own \nresources as per priorities of States.Union Government renders assistance to states \nwhich is technical, advisory, catalytic and promotional in nature.  \nv. Considering the importance of collection of data on coastal processes towards coastal \nprotection measures, a new component \"Coastal Management Information System \n(CMIS)\" was initiated under the Central Sector Plan Scheme \"Development of Water \nResources Information System\". CMIS is a data collection activity carried out to \ncollect near shore coastal data which can be used in planning, design, construction \nand maintenance of site specific coastal protection structures at vulnerable Coastal \nstretches. Establishment of three sites each in the State of Kerala,Tamil Nadu and \nunion territory of Puduchery has been completed. \n \n \n***** \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_131": "GOVERNMENT\n \nOF\n \nINDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nDEPARTMENT\n \nOF\n \nAGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK\n \n \nSABHA\n \nSTARRED\n \nQUESTION\n \nNO.\n \n131\n \nTO\n \nBE\n \nANSWERED\n \nON\n \n2\n6\nTH\n \nJULY\n,\n \n202\n2\n \n \nEFFECT OF HEAT WAVE ON AGRI\n-\nPRODUCE\n \n \n*\n131\n.  \nSHRIMATI RAKSHA NIKHIL \nKHADSE\n:\n \n \nWill\n \nthe\n \nMinister\n \nof\n \n \nAGRICULTURE\n \n \nAND\n \n \nFARMERS\n \nWELFARE\n \nक ृ िष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \nbe\n \np\nle\na\nsed\n \nt\no\n \ns\nt\na\nt\ne\n:\n \n \n(a)\n \nwhether the Government is finding ways and initiating steps to tackle the issue of \nincreasing temperature and heat wave which are \nresulting in lower yield in all agri\n-\nproduce \nthereby affecting the income of the farmers;\n \n(b)\n \n \nif so, the details thereof;\n \n(c)\n \n \nwhether the Government proposes to study the effect of heat wave on the \nagriculture plantation by which certain new diseases are\n \nspreading and spoiling the plants \nthereby affecting the yield of the crops; and\n \n(d)\n \n \nif so, the details thereof?\n \nANSWER\n \n \nTHE\n \nMINISTER\n \nOF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND\n \nFARMERS\n \nWELFARE\n \nक ृ िष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n    \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n \n       \n(a) to (d\n): \n    \nA \nstatement is laid on the table of the House.\n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT\n \nREFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO (d\n) OF LO\nK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 131\n \nDUE FOR ANSWER ON 26\nTH \nJULY, 2022\n \n \n(a\n)\n \nto\n \nd\n):\n \n   \nT\nhe Government is finding ways and initiating steps to tackle the issue \nof \nincreasing temperature and heat wave\n. \nThe DA&FW issues advisories to the States \nthrough the Crop Development Directorates (CDDs) in consultation with State Agricultural \nUniversities (SAUs) / Krishi Vigyan Kendra (KVKs) for creating an awareness campaign\n \nand to implement contingency plans as per local need.  Besides, the India Meteorological \nDepartment under the Ministry of Earth Science\ns\n \nissues weather based operational agro\n-\nmeteorological advisories jointly with ICAR and SAUs under the Gramin Krishi Mau\nsam \nSewa (GKMS) Scheme\n.\n \nAgromet advisories are \nalso \ncommunicated to farmers through \nmultichannel dissemination systems like the print and electronic media, Doordarshan, \nradio, internet etc.  A mobile App viz., ‘Meghdoot\n’ has been launched by the Ministry of \nEarth Sciences, to help farmers to obtain weather information including alerts and related \nagromet advisories specific to their districts. \nThe \nKisan Suvidha \nApp \nof \nMinistry of \nAgriculture and Farmers Welfare\n \nalso issu\nes alert\ns\n \n& advisories to farmers.\n \n \nClimate resilient varieties of seeds in different crops tolerant to climatic stresses \nhave been developed by\n \nthe\n \nIndian Council of Agricultural Research (ICAR). \n177 varieties \nwere evaluated in the farmers field across \nthe country for different climate extremes like \nfloods, droughts, heat wave etc\n.\n \nthat \nwere developed by State Agricultural Universities and \nICAR institutes in various programme. \nICAR has also launched a flagship network project \nNICRA. \n8 climate resilient v\narieties have been developed exclusively under the ‘National \nInnovations in Climate Resilient Agriculture’ (NICRA) programmes.\n \nThe project is being \nimplemented through different components viz., strategic research on adaptation and \nmitigation, demonstratio\nn of technologies on farmers’ fields in 151 clusters of villages one \neach from climatically vulnerable districts and creating awareness among farmers and \nother stakeholders to minimize the climatic change impacts on agriculture. The project \naims to develop\n \nand promote climate resilient technologies that help the districts and \nregions prone to extreme weather conditions like droughts, floods, frost, heat waves, etc., \nto cope with such extremes.\n \n \nIndian Council for Agricultural Research Institute for Dryland \nAgriculture \nCentral \nResearch Institute for Dryland Agriculture \n(ICAR\n-\nCRIDA) formulates District Agriculture \nContingency Plan (DACP) for 650 districts to mitigate the impact of weather related \nchallenges and circulates to all State Agriculture Departments. \nThe plan contains suitable \ntechnology interventions. \n \n\n \nTo address the adverse effect of natural risks on crops, DA&FW ensures \ncomprehensive risk cover for crops of farmers against all non\n-\npreventable natural r\nisks \nincluding heat waves, pest\n \nand diseases\n \nfr\nom pre\n-\nsowing to post \n-\n \nharvest stage under \nPradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojana (PMFBY) which is being implemented in the country \nfrom Kharif 2016. It is \na \nvoluntary scheme for States and farmers. \n \n \nTo deal with long term impacts of Climate Change, the Government of India is \ntaking several steps. \nNational Mission for Sustainable Agriculture (NMSA) is one of the \nMissions within the National Action Plan on Climate Change (NAPCC). The mission aims \nto evo\nlve and implement strategies to make Indian agriculture more resilient to the \nchanging climate. NMSA was approved for three major components i.e. Rainfed Area \nDevelopment (RAD); On Farm Water Management (OFWM); and Soil Health Management \n(SHM). Subsequentl\ny, four new programmes were introduced namely Soil Health Card \n(SHC), Paramparagat Krishi Vikas\n \nYojana (PKVY), Mission Organic Value Chain \nDevelopment in North Eastern Region (MOVCDNER). During 2015\n-\n16, Pradhan Mantri \nKrishi Sinchayee Yojana (PMKSY) was op\nerationalised wherein the OFWM component of \nNMSA was subsumed under Per Drop More Crop (PDMC) component of PMKSY. In \naddition to \nthe \naforementioned programmes under NMSA, \nthe \nRestructured National \nBamboo Mission (NBM) was launched in April 2018.\n \n \nTo study \nthe impact of extreme heat events that \noccurred\n \nrecently \nand to evolve \nsteps\n \nto tackle them the \nICA\nR\n-\nCRIDA\n \nhas conducted a study on \n“Heat Wave 2022 \nCauses, \nImpacts \nand \nWay Forward \nfor Indian Agriculture”.\n \n \nThe study on real time pest dynamics in relation t\no climate variability \nhas been \nconduc\nt\ned in India by ICAR\n-\nCRIDA and ICAR\n-\nNational Centre for Integrated Pest \nManagement (\nNCIPM\n)\n \nunder NICRA.\n \nICAR has adopted \nthe \nNICRA project \nwhich is \noperational\n \nthroughout the country involving almost all SAUs/ ICAR inst\nitutes\n.\n \nImportant \ncrops and pests/ diseases are tak\nen \ncare of and remedies are provided through \na\ngro \nadvisories/ extension services.\n \n*****\n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_132": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF FINANCIAL SERVICES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 132 \nANSWERED ON MONDAY, JULY 18, 2022/ASHADHA 27, 1944 (SAKA) \n \nPM Jan Dhan Yojana \n \n132.   SHRIMATI KESHARI DEVI PATEL: \n \n          Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the number of registered persons under Pradhan Mantri (PM) Jan Dhan \nYojana in Prayagraj in Uttar Pradesh (UP);  \n(b) the number of beneficiaries under the Jan  Dhan Yojana in (UP) including \nPrayagraj; and  \n(c)  the details of the total number of accounts opened in UP including Prayagraj? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE \n \n(DR. BHAGWAT KARAD) \n \n \n  (a) & (b) The total number of Pradhan Mantri Jan Dhan Yojana (PMJDY) account \nholders in  Uttar Pradesh (UP), as on 29.06.2022, is 8.11 crore. This includes      \n29.36 lakh PMJDY account holders from Prayagraj. \n \n \n  \n \n(c) The total number of Operative Current Account Saving Account (CASA) in UP, \nas on 01.07.2022, is 23.93 crore. This includes 67.96 lakh operative CASA account \nholders from Prayagraj. \n***** \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_133": " \n  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK SABHA \n STARRED QUESTION NO. 133 \n  ANSWERED ON 26/07/2022 \n \nRELEASE OF FUNDS UNDER MGNREGS \n \n*133.  SHRI ACHYUTANANDA SAMANTA: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the details of funds that have been released by  the Union Government towards material \nand admin  component for the first quarter of Financial Year 2022 -23 under Mahatma \nGandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme (MGNREGS) including previous \nyear liability, if any, to the Government of Odisha; \n(b)  whether there is any delay in release of the said funds and if so, the reasons therefor; \n(c)  whether the State Government has requested for early release of such material component \nand if so, the details thereof including the timeframe; \n(d)  whether it is a fact that such delay heavily  impacts the creation of livelihood assets as \nwell as its use by beneficiaries who need it most; and \n(e)  if so, the action taken by the Government in this regard? \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SHRI GIRIRAJ SINGH) \n \n \n(a) to (e):       A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n\n \n \nStatement in reply to Parts (a) to (e) of Lok Sabha Starred Question No. 133 \nfor 26.07.2022 \n  \n(a)to(c):   Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme (Mahatma Gandhi \nNREGS) is a demand driven wage employment scheme. Under Mahatma Gandhi NREGS, \nStates furnish fund release proposals to the Government of India. Fund release to the States is a \ncontinuous process and Central Government is committed in making funds available to States for \nthe implementation of the Scheme. \n  \nThe Ministry releases funds periodically in two tranches with each tranche consisting of \none or more installments, keeping in view the “agreed to” Labour Budget, demand for works, \nopening balance, pace of utilization of funds, pending liabilities, overall performance and subject \nto  submission of relevant documents by the State. Also, States can make advance payments \nsubject to recoupment after the release of funds from the Central Government. \n  \nIn the month of March 2022, an amount of Rs. 639.68 crore was released to the State \nGovernment of Odisha for material and administrative components under Mahatma Gandhi \nNREGS and as informed by the State Government, an amount of Rs. 225 crore was available as \nan unspent balance as on 01.04.2022. \n  \nIn the current FY 2022-23, an amount of Rs. 499 crore has been released as first \ninstallment of first tranche for material and administrative components under Mahatma Gandhi \nNREGS to the Government of Odisha.   \n  \n(d)&(e):       Funds were available with State Government in the beginning of the financial year \n2022-23. Also, State Government can make advance payment to State Nodal Agency. The same \ncan be adjusted against future releases by Central Government. \n \n****** ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_134": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.134 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18TH JULY, 2022 \nHISTORIC LOW LABOUR FORCE PARTICIPATION RATE \n \n134.  SHRI GAURAV GOGOI : \n        \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware that Labour Force Participation Rate (LFPR) is \n40.38 per cent; \n(b) if so, whether the Government has studied possible reasons for the historic low \nLFPR; and  \n(c) whether the Government has deliberated policies or other interventions to \nremedy the situation? \n \nANSWER \n MINISTER OF STATE FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) to (c): The data on Employment and Unemployment is collected through Periodic \nLabour Force Survey (PLFS) conducted by the Ministry of Statistics and Programme \nImplementation (MoSPI) since 2017 -18. As per the Annual PLFS Report, the estimated \nLabour Force Participation Rate (LFPR) on usual status for persons of age 15 years and \nabove was 49.8%, 50.2%, 53.5% and 54.9% during 2017 -18, 2018-19, 2019-20 and 2020-\n21, respectively. The data shows that Labour Force Participation Rate has an increasing \ntrend. \n Employment generation coupled with improving employability is the priority of the \nGovernment. Accordingly, the Government of India has taken various steps for \ngenerating employment in the country. The Government of India announced \nAatmanirbhar Bharat package to provide stimulus to business and to mitigate the \nadverse impact of Covid 19. Under this package, the Government is providing fiscal \nstimulus of more than Rupees Twenty Seven lakh crore. This package comprises of \nvarious long term schemes/ programmes/ policies for making the country self-reliant and \nto create employment opportunities.  \n Aatmanirbhar Bharat Rojgar Yojana (ABRY) has been launched with effect from \n1st October, 2020 as part of Atmanirbhar Bharat package 3.0 to incentivize employers \nfor creation of new employment along with social security benefits and restoration of \nloss of employment during Covid-19 pandemic. This scheme being implemented through \nthe Employees’ Provident Fund Organisation (EPFO), seeks to reduce the financial  \n \n\n \nburden of the employers and encourages them to hire more workers. The terminal date \nfor regis tration of beneficiaries has been extended from 30.06.2021 to 31.03.2022. \nBenefits have been provided to 59.53 lakh beneficiaries through 1.50 lakh \nestablishments till 10.07.2022. \n Pradhan Mantri Mudra Yojana (PMMY) is being implemented by the Government \nfor facilitating self-employment. Under PMMY, collateral free loans upto Rs. 10 lakh, are \nextended to micro/small business enterprises and to individuals to enable them to setup \nor expand their business activities. Upto 08.07.2022, 35.94 crore loans were sanctioned \nunder the scheme.  \n PM GatiShakti is a transformative approach for economic growth and sustainable \ndevelopment. The approach is driven by seven engines, namely, Roads, Railways, \nAirports, Ports, Mass Transport, Waterways and Logistics Infrastructu re. This approach \nis powered by Clean Energy and Sabka Prayas leading to huge job and entrepreneurial \nopportunities for all.  \n The Government has put emphasis on railways, roads, urban transport, power, \ntelecom, textiles and affordable housing amid continu ed focus on the National \nInfrastructure Pipeline. Budget 2021 -22 launched Production Linked Incentive (PLI) \nschemes, with an outlay of Rs. 1.97 lakh crore, for a period of 5 years starting from \n2021-22. All these initiatives are expected to collectively ge nerate employment and \nboost output in the medium to long term through multiplier-effects.  \n The Government of India is encouraging various projects involving substantial \ninvestment and public expenditure on schemes like Prime Minister’s Employment \nGeneration Programme (PMEGP), Pt. Deen Dayal Upadhyaya Grameen Kaushalya Yojana \n(DDU-GKY) and Deen Dayal Antodaya Yojana -National Urban Livelihoods Mission (DAY -\nNULM) etc. for employment generation. \n Further, the Ministry of Skill Development and Entrepreneurship (MSDE) is \nimplementing the National Apprenticeship Promotion Scheme (NAPS) and Pradhan \nMantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana (PMKVY) to enhance the employability of youth.  \n Besides these initiatives, various flagship programmes of the Government such as \nMake in Ind ia, Start -up India, Stand -up India, Digital India, Smart City Mission, Atal \nMission for Rejuvenation and Urban Transformation, Housing for All, Infrastructure \nDevelopment and Industrial Corridors are also oriented towards generating employment \nopportunities. \n \n ******  \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_135": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE & FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE \n& FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK\n \nSABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n135\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n26\nTH\n \n \nJULY\n, 2022\n \n \nNATURAL FARMING\n \n \n*135\n.\n \n  \nSHRI CHANDRA SEKHAR BELLANA\n:\n \n \nWill the\n  \n \nMinister \n  \nof \nAGRICULTURE \n \n&\n \n \nFARMERS WELFARE\n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(\na\n)\n \nwhether the Government is aware that agriculture contributes a significant amount to \nthe GDP, reaching the level of even 30% in the previous months; \n \n \n(\nb\n)\n \nif so, the steps taken by the Government to support and encourage natural farming \nin Andhra Pradesh; \n \n \n(\nc\n)\n \nthe budget spent on raising awareness about natural farming amongst farmers \nduring the last three years; \n \n \n(\nd\n)\n \nwhether there are any special natural farming \nschemes specifically for women \nfarmers; and \n \n \n(\ne\n)\n \n \nif so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?\n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER \nOF AGRICULTURE \nAND\n \nFARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n   \n \n(SHRI \nNARENDRA SINGH TOMAR\n)\n \n \n(a) \nto (e): \n  \nA statement is \nlaid on the Table of the House.\n \n \n \n \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS\n \n(a) to (e) \nOF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 135 DUE FOR REPLY ON 26\nTH \nJULY, 2022.\n \n \n(a):     \nAs per Provisional Estimates of National Income, released by National Statistical \nOffic\ne (NSO), Ministry of Statistics and Programme Implementation on 31\nst\n \nMay 2022, the \nshare of Agriculture and Allied Sectors in Gross Value Added (GVA) of the country\n \nin\n \n2021\n-\n22 is 18.6%. \n \n \n(b) \nto (e)\n:\n \nGovernment is promoting Natural Farming through Bhartiya\n \nPrakritik Krishi \nPadhati (BPKP) introduced during 2020\n-\n21 as a sub scheme of Paramparagat Krishi Vikas \nYojana (PKVY) for the promotion of traditional indigenous practices including Natural \nFarming . The scheme mainly emphasises on exclusion of all synthet\nic chemical inputs and \npromotes on\n-\nfarm biomass recycling with major stress on biomass mulching, use of cow \ndung\n-\nurine formulations and other plant\n-\nbased preparations. Under BPKP, financial \nassistance of Rs 12200/ha for 3 years is provided for cluster form\nation, capacity building and \ncontinuous handholding by trained personnel, certification and residue analysis. Until now, \nunder natural farming an area of 4.09 lakh ha area has been covered and a total fund of Rs. \n4980.99 lakh has been released to 8 States\n \nacross the country.\n \n \nSo far, in Andhra Pradesh Rs. 750 lakh has been released to the State to bring \n100000 ha \narea under Natural Farming and Annual Action Plan for 150000 ha area has been \napproved. \n \n \nMANAGE is designated as ”Knowledge partner for Natural F\narming extension” and is \ninitiating training of Master Trainers and sensitization of  30,000 Gram Pradhans through 750 \nonline workshops from  April to 15th August 2022\n  \nand in 2021\n-\n22 \nan \namount of Rs 175.75 \nlakh has been released. \nDuring current year, \nMANAGE has organized 647 awareness \ncourses and exposed 38762 Gram Pradhans to Natural Farming. National Centre of Organic \nFarming\n \nhas been \nreconstituted as National Centre for Org\nanic and Natural Farming \n(NCONF)\n \nto promote chemical free farming including n\natural farming in the country through \ncapacity building of stakeholders.\n \nNCONF has \ntrained 7100 farmers through 37 trainings \nprogrammes. \nDA&FW released Rs 11.94 crore to initiate trainings of farmers through 425 \nKVKS which will be model training and demons\ntration centres\n.\n \n72 TV programmes and 42 \nRadio programmes have been done on Natural Farming.  \n \n \nThere are no special natural farming schemes specificall\ny for women\n \nfarmers as \nnatural farming is not gender specific scheme. \nThe State\n-\nwise details of funds \nreleased and \narea un\nder BPKP is given at \nAnnexure\n.\n \n \n\n \nAnnexure \n \n \nAnnexure referred in reply to part (b) to (e) of Lok Sabha Starred Q. No. *135 due for \nanswer on 26/07/2022\n \n \nThe State\n-\nwise details of funds released and area covered under Bhartiya \nPrakritik \nKrishi Padhthi (BPKP)\n \n \n \nSl.No.\n \nStates\n \nReleased date\n \nArea in Ha\n \nAmount Released\n \n(in lakh)\n \n1.\n \nAndhra Pradesh\n \n30.09\n.\n20\n \n100000\n \n750.00\n \n2.\n \nChhattisgarh\n \n09.12.20\n \n85000\n \n1352.52\n \n3.\n \nKerala\n \n30.09.20\n \n84000\n \n1336.60\n \n4.\n \nHimachal Pradesh\n \n24.12.20\n \n12000\n \n286.42\n \n5.\n \nJharkhand\n \n02.12.20\n \n3400\n \n54.10\n \n6.\n \nOdisha\n \n02.02.21\n \n24000\n \n381.89\n \n7.\n \nMadhya Pradesh\n \n16.02.21\n \n99000\n \n787.64\n \n8.\n \nTamil Nadu\n \n16.02.21\n \n2000\n \n31.82\n \nTotal\n \n \n409400\n \n4980.99\n \n \n \n*****\n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_136": "����������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n��������������������\n����������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n����������������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n����������������������������������������������������������������������\n�� ������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n�������� �������� ���������������� ������ ������������ ������������ ������������ ������ �������� ���������������������� ������ �������������� ���������� ��������\n������������������������ ���������������� ������ �������� ���������������� ������ �������� ������������������ ���������������� ������ �������� ��������������\n��������������������������������������������\n�������� ������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������\n�������� ������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������� ��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��\nANSWER \nMINISTER OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING; AND \nMINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS {SHRI ANURAG \nSINGH THAKUR} \n \n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n******** \n \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (d) OF \nLOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. *136 FOR ANSWER ON \n26.07.2022 \n��\n(a) to (d) Ministry of Information and Broadcasting disseminates \ninformation on Government policies, programmes, initiatives and \nachievements through its various media units like Press \nInformation Bureau (PIB), Central Bureau of Communication \n(CBC, erstwhile BOC) and Prasar Bharati, the public broadcaster, \nthrough Doordarshan and All India Radio, utilizing various media \nvehicles like print, electronic media, social media, internet \nwebsites, outdoor publicity, live arts and interpersonal media. \n \nDuring the months from April - June 2022, Ministry of Information \nand Broadcasting initiated a comprehensive Public Information \nCampaign on the theme of ‘Seva, Sushashan aur Gareeb Kalyan’ \nhighlighting a variety of Government schemes and programmes \nincluding those for upliftment of weaker sections of the society.  \n \nAlso the Media Units of the Ministry of Information and \nBroadcasting played a crucial role in conducting awareness \ncampaigns through people’s participation against COVID-19.  \nThe Government organized extensive outdoor and interpersonal \ncommunication as part of a special Jan Andolan campaign with \nactive participation of Frontline Workers, Anganwadis and \nPanchayati Raj Institutions, for awareness of Covid appropriate \nbehavior along with several institutions and bodies of the \nCentral and State Governments, under the overall campaign \ntheme of #Unite2FightCorona, with emphasis on COVID-19 \nappropriate behaviour. \n\nCommunication through printing and distribution of New India \nSamachar, a fortnightly publication, up to the level of Gram \nPanchayat is also a medium of informing people to the last mile \nabout the ongoing and new programmes and policies of \nGovernment of India. Beside this, e-version of New India \nSamachar is being sent to approx. 7 crore readers in flip book as \nwell as PDF format in 13 languages via emails using E-Sampark \nplatform of MyGov India. \n \nAs a part of the initiative for inclusive governance, Dooordarshan \nhas introduced simultaneous sign language interpretation during \nthe live telecast of Press Conference related to Cabinet \ndecisions.   \n \nMyGov, Government of India’s Citizen Engagement Platform, in \ncollaboration with various Ministries and Departments, engages \npeople in policy formulation and seeks opinion of people on \nissues/ topics of public interest and welfare, and also \ndisseminates information on various Government schemes and \nprogrammes, with multiple engagement methodologies such as \ndiscussions, tasks, polls, surveys, blogs, talks, quizzes and on-\nground activities by innovatively using various technology. \n \nThe Government also collaborates with private media, including \nprivate Satellite TV channel, private FM and Community radio \nStation in awareness generation. \n  \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_137": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 137 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.07.2022 \n \nEcological Sensitive Areas \n \n137.    SHRI KODIKUNNIL SURESH: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)  whether the Government is intending to release the final notification identifying and \ndemarcating Ecologically Sensitive Areas as per the recommendations contained in Dr. \nKasturirangan Panel Report and if so, the details thereof; \n(b)  whether the final status of Ecologically Sensitive Areas with respect to States of Kerala \nand Karnataka has been finalized; \n(c)  whether the Government has been formally communicated by Kerala and Karnataka \nState Governments regarding the concerns pertaining to the recommendations contained \nin the Dr. KasturiRangan Report adversely affecting the livelihood of people in the \nregion; \n(d)  if so, whether the Government call for a revised estimation of recommendation of the \nReport in order to allay the concerns raised by various States including Kerala where a \nsignificant population resides in and around the proposed buffer zones; and \n(e)  if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE \nCHANGE  \n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY) \n \n(a) to (e) With a view to issue final notification to declare Ecologically Sensitive Areas (ESA) \nof the Western Ghats, on the basis of Kasturirangan Panel Report,this Ministry had issued draft \nNotification vide S.O. 733 (E) on 10.02.2014, with an eco-sensitive area of 56,825 km2 \nspreading over six States, namely, Goa, Gujarat, Karnataka, Kerala, Maharashtra and Tamil \nNadu. \n \nWith persisting demand from the States to reduce/alter the ESA area from the \nrecommendation of the Kasturirangan Panel (High Level Working Group) Report, the final \nNotification for Western Ghat Eco-sensitive Area could not be issued so far. While consistent \nefforts to bring the 6 States on board has been underway, the Ministry has subsequently, \nrepublished the draft Notification four times, vide S.O. 2435 (E) dated 04.09.2015; S.O. 667 \n(E) dated 27.02.2017; S.O. 5135 (E) dated 03.10.2018 and S.O. 3072 (E) dated 06.07.2022 with \n\n \n \nsame ESA area. Presently, the recent draft notification dated 06.07.2022is under Stakeholder \nconsultation for mandatory 60 days. \n \nIn order to follow a coherent and consistent approach in Notifying the ESA of Western \nGhats, this Ministry has been interacting with all the States of the Western Ghats region at \nvarious levels. Efforts are being made to finalise the notification taking into consideration the \nenvironmental concerns of the region and the suggestions and/or recommendations of the \nconcerned State Governments including Governments of Kerala and Karnatakain a holistic \nmanner, keeping in view the conservation aspects of the disaster prone pristine ecosystem, and \nthe rights, privileges, needs and developmental aspirations of the region.  \n \nTo address the issues raised by the state Governments, MoEFCC has already constituted \na Committee to examine the concerns/suggestions of the six state Governments.  \n \n**** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_138": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPART\nMENT OF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n138\n \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n26\nTH \nJuly\n, 202\n2\n \n \nLIME CULTIVATION\n \n \n   \n \n*\n138\n.     SHRI\n \nM.V.V. SATYANARAYANA\n:\n \n                \n \nSHRI \nKURUVA GORANTLA MADHAV\n:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n            \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(\na\n)\n \nwhether the Government\n \nis aware that excessive rainfall in November and December \n2021 destroyed lime crop in Andhra Pradesh;\n \n(\nb\n)\n \nif so, the steps taken to support farmers during this difficult time;\n \n(\nc\n)\n \nwhether any Central compensation has been provided to the lime growers;\n \n(\nd\n)\n \nif so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor;\n \n(\ne\n)\n \nwhether the Government is aware that lack of proper prices in the last seven to eight years \nfor lime has led farmers to avoid its cultivation; and\n \n(\nf\n)\n \nif so, the steps taken to redress the situation \nand encourage its cultivation\n \n?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n   \n       \n \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n \n \n \n(a) to\n \n(\nf\n):    \nA statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRE\nD TO IN REPLY TO \nPARTS (a) TO (f\n) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO.\n138\n \nDUE\n \nFOR REPLY ON \n26\nTH\n \nJULY\n, 202\n2\n.\n \n(\na\n) to (d\n): \n     \nYes\n.\n \nA\nn area of \n227.71 Hectares of Lime crops\n \n(\n91.71 Hectares of Sweet Orange \nand 136.00 Hectares of Acid Lime\n)\n \nwas damaged due to Heavy Rains in November 2021\n \nas \nper \nthe information received from the State Government\n.\n \nDistrict wise details are \ngiven \nat \nAnnexure\n-\nI\n.\n \nThe State Government is primarily responsible for providing necessary relief \nmeasures in the wake of natural calamities, including flood. For undertaking relief measures, \nfunds are available with the State Government in the form of State Disaster Response \nFund \n(SDRF). Additional financial assistance, over and above SDRF, is considered from the National \nDisaster Response Fund (NDRF) for natural calamities of a \nsevere \nnature and is approved on \nthe basis of a Memorandum received from the State Government, in a\nccordance with \nestablished procedures. Thereafter, an Inter\n-\nMinisterial Central Team (IMCT) is constituted and \ndeputed for an on\n-\nthe\n-\nspot assessment of damage and requirement of funds for relief \noperations, as per the extant items and norms. The report of \nthe IMCT is considered by the Sub \nCommittee of the National Executive Committee (SC\n-\nNEC). Thereafter, the High Level \nCommittee (HLC) approves the final quantum of financial assistance.\n \nAccordingly, as per information received from the State Government, a\nn \namount of \nRs.18.34 Lakh has been released to 126 f\na\nrmers \nf\nor Sweet Orange \nand an amount of Rs. \n27.20 Lakh released to 272 farmers \nfor Acid Lime \nby\n \nD\nirect \nB\nenefit \nT\nransfer \n(DBT)\n \nmode \nthrough\n \nRevenue Disaster Management Department, Government of Andhra Pradesh\n.\n \n \n(\ne\n)\n \n& \n(\nf\n):  \n   \nNo. \nT\nhe area under cultivation of Lime \ncrops \n(\nAcid Lime \nand\n \nSweet Orange\n) \nduring \nthe last \nsix\n \nyears has been increasing\n \nas per the information received from the State \nGovernment\n. The area of Acid Lime has increased from \n34,264 Hectares\n \n(\n2015\n-\n16\n)\n \nto 43,044 \nHectares\n \n(2020\n-\n21). Similarly, for Sweet Orange, the area has increased from \n77,940 Hectares\n \n(\n2015\n-\n16\n) \nto 1\n,\n10\n,\n970 Hectares \n(\n2020\n-\n21\n)\n.\n  \n \n \n \nAn area of 7705 Hectares has been covered under Sweet Orange & Acid Lime \ncultivation in Andhra Pradesh \nunder Area\n \nExpansion component of Mission for Integrated \nDevelopment of Horticulture (MIDH) scheme \nsince 2016\n-\n17. \n \nThe year wise details \nfor\n \narea \nexpansi\non taken in Andhra Pradesh are given in \nAnnexure\n-\nII\n.\n \n \n \n\n \nAnnexure\n-\nI\n \n \nThe district\n-\nwise area damaged due to Heavy Rains in November\n-\n2021\n \nS.No\n \nDistrict\n \nAcid Lime\n \nDistrict\n \nSweet Orange\n \nArea \nin Ha\n \nInput \nSubsidy \n(Rs.in \nLakh)\n \nNo. of \nfarmers \naffected\n \nArea \nin \nHa\n \nInput \nSubsidy \n(Rs.in \nLakh)\n \nNo. of \nfarmers \naffected\n \n1\n \nYSR \nKadapa\n \n120.76\n \n24.15\n \n234\n \nYSR \nKadapa\n \n88.21\n \n17.64\n \n121\n \n2\n \nSPSR \nNellore\n \n11.90\n \n2.38\n \n32\n \nAnanthapur\n \n3.50\n \n0.70\n \n5\n \n3\n \nPrakasam\n \n2.00\n \n0.40\n \n4\n \n---\n \n---\n \n---\n \n---\n \n4\n \nKurnool\n \n1.20\n \n0.24\n \n1\n \n---\n \n---\n \n---\n \n---\n \n5\n \nChittoor\n \n0.15\n \n0.03\n \n1\n \n---\n \n---\n \n---\n \n---\n \n \nTotal\n \n136.01\n \n27.2\n0\n \n272\n \nTotal\n \n91.71\n \n18.34\n \n126\n \n \n \n \n\n \nAnnexure\n-\nII\n \nArea Expansion under Mission for Integrated \nDevelopment of \nHorticulture (MIDH)\n \nSweet Orange/\n \nAcid\n \nLime\n \nCultivation\n \n \nYear\n \nPhysical\n \n \nTarget \nin \nHa\n \n \n \nAchievement\n \n        \n \n        \nin\n \nHa\n \n \nRemarks\n \n2016\n-\n17\n \n1656\n \n1550\n \n---\n \n2017\n-\n18\n \n0\n \n0\n \nGovernment of India during\n \n2017\n-\n18\n \nhave allotted\n \nonly\n \nMango under perennial\n \ncrops\n \n2018\n-\n19\n \n962\n \n940\n \n---\n \n2019\n-\n20\n \n2222\n \n2150\n \n---\n \n2020\n-\n21\n \n2000\n \n1850\n \n---\n \n2021\n-\n22\n \n1500\n \n1215\n \n---\n \nTotal\n \n8340\n \n7705\n \n---\n \n \n \n******\n ",
