���������� �������� ���� ����������� ���������\n���������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������ �\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������� �\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������\n������� ������� ���� ����� ������������ ���� ����������� ������ ���������\n������������ ������������������ �\n�\n������� �",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_197": " \n \n GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 197 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.07.2022 \n \nPublic Consultation in Formulation in NEP, 2020 \n \n197. SHRI S. JAGATHRAKSHAKAN: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the new National Education Policy (NEP), 2020 was formulated after one of the \nlargest public consultation in policy history;  \n \n(b) if so, whether despite wide consultation and mass contributions, the policy lacks clear \narticulation of outcomes and evolution of the policy;  \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof; and  \n \n(d) the remedial steps taken or proposed to be taken by the Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION  \n(DR. SUBHAS SARKAR) \n \n(a) to (d): The National Education Policy 2020 has been finalised after a highly participative, \ninclusive and multi-pronged consultation process, through online, grassroots and thematic \nexpert consultations, involving all stakeholders. A large number of consultations / deliberations \nwere held with different stakeholders viz-a-viz State/UT Governments, Government of India \nMinistries, Members of Parliament, Educational Institutions, Industries, public etc.  \n \n The National Education Policy 2020 (NEP 2020) provides different timelines as well as \nprinciples and methodology for its implementation. As per NEP 2020, implementation of the \nPolicy requires multiple initiatives and actions, which will have to be taken by multiple bodies \nin a synchronized and systematic manner. Since education is a concurrent subject, it needs \ncareful planning, joint monitoring and collaborative implementation between the Centre and \nStates. State/UT Governments play a vital role in the implementation of the National Education \nPolicy 2020.  \n \n From time to time, a series of workshops/ seminars/meetings have been held with States/UTs \nand other stakeholders to discuss innovative ideas for NEP implementation. In this direction, \nrecently a National Conference of Chief Secretaries was held from 15th to 17th June 2022 inter-\nalia to discuss the implementation of various aspects of National Education Policy 2020 with \nState/UT Governments. Also, an Akhil Bharatiya Shiksha Samagam was organised from 7th to \n9th July 2022 to discuss various themes of NEP 2020 for higher education. In this Samagam, \nVice Chancellors of Central/State/Deemed/Private Universities, Directors / Heads of Higher \nEducational Institutions, Principals / Faculties of Colleges, Educationists, Policy-makers etc \nparticipated.  \n \n*******  ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_198": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CORPORATE AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 198 \n ANSWERED ON MONDAY, JULY 18, 2022/ \nASHADHA 27, 1944 (SAKA)  \n \nCSR SPENDING  \nQUESTION \n198. SHRI LAVU SRI KRISHNA DEVARAYALU: \n        DR. BEESETTI VENKATA SATYAVATHI: \n        SHRI MADDILA GURUMOORTHY: \n        SHRIMATI GODDETI MADHAVI: \n        SHRI CHANDRA SEKHAR BELLANA: \n \n Will the Minister of CORPORATE AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the details of the Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR) spending during the last five years, \nState and Sector-wise; \n \n(b) whether it is true that local spending as part of CSR funds is being neglected and CSR \nspending is concentrated in a few States/Sectors; \n \n(c) if so, whether the Ministry has taken any steps to ensure equitable distribution of such \nCSR spending amongst different States and Sectors as also to increase local spending \nunder CSR; \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and \n \n(e) whether the Ministry is planning to make a statutory amendment in this regard, if so, the \ndetails thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE MINISTRY OF STATISTICS AND \nPROGRAMME IMPLEMENTATION; MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE \nMINISTRY OF PLANNING AND MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF CORPORATE \nAFFAIRS                                                                 \n   [RAO INDERJIT SINGH] \n \n(a):  The broad framework for Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR) has been provided under \nSection 135 of the Companies Act, 2013 (‘Act’), Schedule VII of the Act and Companies (CSR \nPolicy) Rules, 2014. The CSR framework is disclosure based and CSR mandated com panies \nare required to file details of CSR activities annually in the MCA21 registry.  All data related \nto CSR filed by companies in MCA21 registry is available in public domain and can be \naccessed at www.csr.gov.in. On the basis of the filings made by the companies in the MCA21 \nregistry, State /UT-wise and development sector -wise details of CSR spent by all the \ncompanies during last five Financial Years (FY) 2016-17, 2017-18, 2018-19, 2019-20 and 2020-\n21 are at Annexure – I and Annexure – II respectively. Further, the companies are required to \nfile the CSR data for the financial year 2021-22 on or before 31.03.2023.  \nCond….2/ \n\n \n \n- 2 – \n \n(b) to (e): An analysis of CSR data during the period 2014-15 to 2020-21, reveals that around \n33% of the total CSR spent by the companies is in the states of Maharashtra, Karnataka, \nGujarat, Andhra Pradesh and Tamil Nadu. Similarly, around 60% of the total CSR spent by the \ncompanies is in the areas of education, healthcare and rural development related activities.  \nThe 2nd proviso to Section 135 (5) of the Act provides that the company shall give preference \nto the local area and areas around it where it operates. In order to ensure an all India spread \nof CSR expenditure, the Ministry vide General Circular no. 14/2021 dated 25.08.2021  had \nclarified that the emphasis on local area is only directory and not mandatory in nature and \ncompanies need to balance local area preference with national  priorities. It was further \nclarified that the activities mentioned in Schedule VII are br oad based and can be \ninterpreted liberally. Under the Act, CSR is a Board driven process and the Board of the \ncompany is empowered to plan, decide, execute and monitor CSR activities of the company \nbased on the recommendation of its CSR Committee. The Government does not issue any \nspecific direction to the companies to spend in any particular geographical area or activity. \n******* \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure - I \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 198 FOR 18.07.2022   \n State/UT-wise CSR Expenditure (in Rs. Cr.) \nS.N. State/UT FY 2016-17 FY 2017-18 FY 2018-19 FY 2019-20  FY 2020-21 \n1  Andaman And Nicobar  0.63 0.73 0.82 1.29 2.19 \n2  Andhra Pradesh  741.52 575.07 664.87 710.00 662.39 \n3  Arunachal Pradesh  24.05 11.91 24.56 18.02 10.58 \n4  Assam  256.92 211.33 210.00 282.14 163.21 \n5  Bihar  100.62 106.17 137.57 110.15 78.02 \n6  Chandigarh  21.96 20.51 11.46 15.58 13.12 \n7  Chhattisgarh  84.66 176.70 149.35 268.18 305.73 \n8  Dadra And Nagar Haveli  7.37 6.98 13.48 18.34 18.08 \n9  Daman And Diu  2.63 20.23 6.25 9.53 5.25 \n10  Delhi  460.25 579.37 749.24 827.74 657.73 \n11  Goa  33.28 53.77 46.77 43.86 40.95 \n12  Gujarat  864.83 967.97 1,082.09 982.53 1,397.26 \n13  Haryana  386.17 363.43 377.53 521.11 536.86 \n14  Himachal Pradesh  22.83 69.23 78.79 78.61 104.60 \n15  Jammu And Kashmir  42.65 50.77 36.44 25.27 35.10 \n16  Jharkhand  95.49 109.23 109.80 155.21 208.34 \n17  Karnataka  875.38 1,145.42 1,250.39 1,445.80 1,205.23 \n18  Kerala  133.82 219.71 354.67 295.78 283.78 \n19  Lakshadweep  - 2.27 0.39 - 0.01 \n20  Madhya Pradesh  161.11 163.92 243.17 215.06 344.13 \n21  Maharashtra  2,414.80 2,797.53 3,144.23 3,336.14 3,306.72 \n22  Manipur  12.35 4.81 7.81 14.21 9.66 \n23  Meghalaya  9.75 11.18 16.54 17.65 12.46 \n24  Mizoram  0.08 1.28 0.11 0.25 0.81 \n25  Nagaland  0.53 1.81 2.12 5.10 3.57 \n26  Odisha  316.31 504.22 688.25 714.82 547.57 \n27  Puducherry  7.43 6.09 9.15 11.32 11.79 \n28  Punjab  75.05 112.36 166.00 188.52 126.01 \n29  Rajasthan  324.23 443.35 595.44 733.95 643.07 \n30  Sikkim  6.71 7.00 5.87 10.99 15.15 \n31  Tamil Nadu  544.43 669.65 876.83 1,069.45 1,082.33 \n32  Telangana  256.15 380.56 428.06 445.56 579.75 \n33  Tripura  1.25 1.88 23.06 9.40 9.29 \n34  Uttar Pradesh  321.23 435.21 519.14 577.08 826.67 \n35  Uttarakhand  102.37 85.79 172.26 124.65 151.30 \n36  West Bengal  275.68 338.32 381.77 416.97 427.44 \n37  NEC/ Not mentioned* 7.63 137.93 4.44 30.43 177.51 \n38  PAN India ** 4,565.50 5,505.31 6,428.09 9,373.03 7,490.84 \n39 \nContribution to Funds  \nincluded in Schedule VII  \nof the Act \n787.22 799.18 1,155.25 1,787.89 3,371.00 \n Grand Total (in Cr.)  14,344.87 17,098.18 20,172.07 24,891.63 24,865.46 \n(Data up to 31.03.2022)   [Source: National CSR Data Portal] \n*Companies did not specify the names of States where projects were undertaken \n** Companies indicated more than one State where projects were undertaken. \n  \n****** \n\n \nAnnexure - II \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 198 FOR 18.07.2022   \nDevelopment Sector-wise CSR Expenditure (in Rs. Cr.) \nS. No Development Sector FY  \n2016-17  \nFY  \n2017-18  \nFY  \n2018-19  \nFY  \n2019-20  \nFY  \n2020-21  \n1 Agro Forestry 43.45 66.79 64.75 67.38 18.87 \n2 Animal Welfare 78.70 63.52 98.28 106.07 187.70 \n3 Armed Forces, Veterans, \nWar Widows/ Dependants 37.86 29.09 90.18 62.04 83.15 \n4 Art And Culture 305.57 395.22 225.08 933.31 479.63 \n5 Clean Ganga Fund 24.37 33.96 8.11 6.63 13.36 \n6 Conservation Of Natural \nResources 119.09 228.14 173.55 159.24 85.03 \n7 Education 4,505.05 5,763.08 6,093.63 7,164.49 6,391.86 \n8 Environmental \nSustainability 1,076.46 1,301.96 1,364.21 1,468.22 981.78 \n9 Gender Equality 72.60 24.01 51.81 82.93 35.27 \n10 Health Care 2,491.52 2,776.93 3,608.57 4,892.28 6,946.75 \n11 Livelihood Enhancement \nProjects 515.47 832.40 907.49 1,077.47 796.14 \n12 Other Central Government \nFunds 419.99 292.73 730.96 930.86 1,541.56 \n13 Poverty, Eradicating \nHunger, Malnutrition 606.55 811.20 1,190.66 1,149.10 1,236.09 \n14 Prime Minister's National \nRelief Fund 158.80 200.42 320.67 797.03 1,656.40 \n15 Rural Development Projects 1,554.78 1,724.07 2,430.86 2,291.38 1,818.38 \n16 Safe Drinking Water 149.69 220.87 228.10 253.00 197.05 \n17 Sanitation 421.80 460.68 506.66 521.64 326.11 \n18 Senior Citizens Welfare 27.75 40.09 44.97 52.18 55.13 \n19 Setting Up Homes And \nHostels For Women 62.22 70.58 57.01 48.50 39.92 \n20 Setting Up Orphanage 16.80 39.87 12.89 36.47 15.43 \n21 Slum Area Development 51.49 39.16 51.06 42.94 74.59 \n22 Socio-Economic \nInequalities 148.01 155.95 167.84 214.79 119.18 \n23 Special Education 165.33 140.01 186.08 196.81 202.05 \n24 Swachh Bharat Kosh 184.06 272.07 95.50 53.37 159.68 \n25 Technology Incubators 23.09 16.94 32.10 53.50 61.99 \n26 Training To Promote Sports 180.33 285.41 308.87 302.12 240.90 \n27 Vocational Skills 373.46 546.46 798.21 1,165.22 630.78 \n28 Women Empowerment 141.62 251.37 236.34 259.27 188.04 \n29 NEC/ Not Mentioned* 388.96 15.20 87.61 503.40 282.65 \nGrand Total (in Cr.) 14,344.87 17,098.18 20,172.07 24,891.63 24,865.46 \n   (Data upto 31.03.2022) [Source: National CSR Data Portal] \n \n      * Companies  either did not specify the names of sectors or indicated more than one sector where \nprojects were undertaken. \n******* ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_199": " \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 199 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 29TH JULY, 2022 \n \nAYUSHMAN BHARAT DIGITAL MISSION \n \n*199. SHRI SUNIL KUMAR SINGH:  \nMS. DIYA KUMARI:  \n \nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to \nstate:  \n \n(a) the details of steps taken by the Government towards infrastructure and \nadministrative development to support the Ayushman Bharat Digital Mission in the \ncountry;  \n \n(b) the details of role played by States and the private players in furthering various \ninitiatives under the scheme;  \n \n(c) the achievements made under the scheme till date, State/UT-wise particularly in \nJharkhand;  \n \n(d) whether the Government has taken an y steps to provide benefits of the scheme \nto the poor and those affected by digital divide, especially in areas of Jharkhand \nwhich lack internet connectivity; and  \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE  \n(DR MANSUKH MANDAVIYA) \n \n(a) to ( e) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 199* FOR 29TH JULY, 2022 \n \n(a) to (e) Government of India has launched Ayushman  Bharat Digital Mission \n(ABDM) which aims to create a platform enabling interoperability of health data \nwithin the health ecosystem to create longitudinal Electronic Health Record (EHR) \nof every citizen.  \n \nThe infrastructure required for development of interoperable digital platform \n& certain modules under the National Digital Health Mission (now Ayushman \nBharat Digital Mission) has broadly been assessed and identified in line with the \nobjectives of ABDM.  \n \nFor administrative support, various policy framewo rks such as Health Data \nManagement Policy, Sandbox Guidelines, Health Information Provider Guidelines \netc. have been prepared and issued for smooth implementation of ABDM.  \nStates/UTs are expected to play a crucial role in adoption of ABDM. ABDM \nbeing a centrally sponsored scheme, financial assistance is provided to States/UTs \nfor Human resources, Information Education & Communication (IEC) and \nCapacity building. States/UTs administration will help in onboarding and \nhandholding all the facilities/health pro fessionals, integrating any state health \nprogrammes and state specific digital health solution(s) with ABDM and to \ncollaborate with various stakeholders of digital ecosystem to increase the creation \nand seamless sharing of digital health records.  \nBoth public and private sector IT solutions in health are integrated through \nvarious registries under ABDM to aid creation of longitudinal EHR across the \ncountry. Till 23 rd July 2022, 47 entities (17 public and 30 private) have \nsuccessfully integrated with ABDM ec o system. More than 800 entities \n(government and private) are presently integrating with ABDM. \n \n\n \n \nAs on 23rd July 2022, a total 23,08,75,100 ABHA Numbers; 1,10,939 Health \nFacilities and 30,210 Healthcare Professionals have been registered (State/UT-wise \ndetails attached at Annexure - A). \n \nDistrict wise detail of ABHA numbers, Healthcare facility & Healthcare \nProfessionals registered in Jharkhand are attached as Annexure - B.  \n \nGovernment has taken various steps to ensure that the benefits of the \nmission reach every citizen. Inclusion is one of the key principles of ABDM. The \ndigital health ecosystem created by ABDM supports continuity of care across \nprimary, secondary and tertiary healthcare in a seamless manner. It aids availability \nof health care servic es particularly in remote and rural areas through various \ntechnology interventions like telemedicine etc. The Mission has provisions for \nassisted mode in places where internet connectivity might be poor. For instance, \nOffline mode for creation of ABHA has been enabled wherever internet \nconnectivity might be poor or there would be unavailability of hardware or both. \n**** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nAnnexure A \nStates/UTs wise details of ABHA/HFR/HPR Status \n                                                                                                                      (As on 23rd July 2022) \nS. No. Name of the States \n/ UTs \nABHA Created* \n \nHealth \nFacilities \nRegistered \nHealth \nProfessionals \nRegistered \n1 Andhra Pradesh 3,25,89,804 26,824 14,508 \n2 Uttar Pradesh 1,93,56,779 13,349 3,018 \n3 Bihar 1,64,56,628 12,791 2,675 \n4 Madhya Pradesh 1,54,79,136 12,379 1,817 \n5 Maharashtra 1,48,25,017 11,977 1,787 \n6 Kerala 1,30,39,922 10,750 1,403 \n7 Chhattisgarh 1,27,14,061 8,593 978 \n8 West Bengal 1,11,60,466 5,961 542 \n9 Gujarat 1,04,38,163 1,841 482 \n10 Odisha 99,90,477 1,574 480 \n11 Rajasthan 70,00,827 627 477 \n12 Karnataka 67,46,527 543 363 \n13 Delhi 63,47,433 456 345 \n14 Punjab 50,36,024 441 262 \n15 Jharkhand 46,92,147 380 236 \n16 Jammu And \nKashmir 44,03,448 372 169 \n17 Haryana 43,62,627 371 123 \n18 Telangana 40,68,573 311 88 \n19 Tamil Nadu 31,25,795 288 73 \n20 Uttarakhand 25,89,842 244 72 \n21 Himachal Pradesh 18,02,408 221 60 \n22 Assam 10,93,296 200 57 \n23 Puducherry 8,76,485 177 33 \n24 Chandigarh 5,33,750 143 32 \n25 DNHDD 5,20,506 49 29 \n26 Manipur 4,58,373 47 29 \n27 Andaman And \nNicobar Islands 4,20,474 25 24 \n28 Tripura 3,65,228 5 13 \n29 Goa 3,35,327 0 12 \n30 Sikkim 3,10,551 0 9 \n31 Mizoram 2,99,561 0 5 \n32 Nagaland 2,66,408 0 2 \n33 Ladakh 2,60,266 0 2 \n34 Arunachal Pradesh 2,02,250 0 2 \n35 Meghalaya 1,34,960 0 2 \n36 Lakshadweep 72,003 0 1 \n*1,84,99,558 ABHA numbers were created through the CoWin portal. The details of the states \nagainst various ABHAs has not been populated as it is no mandatory field. \n\n \nAnnexure B \n \nDistrict wise details of ABHA/HFR/HPR Status in Jharkhand \n                                     