    "ls|LS17_S9_U_234": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HEAVY INDUSTRIES  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 234 \nANSWERED ON 19.07.2022 \n \nINCIDENTS OF FIRE IN ELECTRIC VEHICLES  \n \n234. DR. SUKANTA MAJUMDAR:  \nDR. JAYANTA KUMAR ROY: \nSHRI VINOD KUMAR SONKAR: \nSHRI RAJA AMARESHWARA NAIK:  \nSHRI N. REDDEPPA:  \nSHRI RAVNEET SINGH BITTU:  \nSHRI BHOLA SINGH:  \nSHRIMATI SANGEETA KUMARI SINGH DEO:  \nSHRI RAJVEER SINGH (RAJU BHAIYA):  \nSHRI P.V. MIDHUN REDDY:  \nSHRI THIRUNAVUKKARASAR SU:  \n \nWill the Minister of HEAVY INDUSTRIES भारी उद्योग मंत्री be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether numerous scary fire incidents have been reported involving electric \nscooters/electric vehicles resulting in loss of lives and injury in various parts of the country \nthereby threatening the safety of commuters during the last six months and if so, the details \nthereof, month-wise and State-wise;  \n \n(b) whether the Government anticipates any slump in the growth rate and supply of \nElectric Vehicles (EVs) due to these incidents in the future and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether the Government is taking any measures including adequate testing facilities \nfor imported components for EVs to ensure their assimilation with domestic conditions and  if \nso, the details thereof; \n \n(d) whether the Government has constituted any expert committee to enquire into the \nabove issues and for making any recommendations on remedial steps, to recall the whole lot \nof vehicles, action against the defective manufacturers and to ensure adequate compensation \nfor the mishaps; \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and \n \n(f) whether the Government has issued necessary orders on the defaulting companies and \nif so, the details thereof including the other steps being taken in this regard so far? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR HEAVY INDUSTRIES \n(SHRI KRISHAN PAL GURJAR) \n \n  \n\n \n(a):  As per information available, fire incidents involving electric scooters/ electric \nvehicles, are as under: - \nMonth State \nMarch- 2022 Tamil Nadu \nApril 2022 Andhra Pradesh \n \n(b) to (c)):  As per vahan portal, there is no downtrend in the sale of EVs.  Testing of \ncomponents for EVs is done as per relevant standards, as specified in Rule 126 of the Central \nMotor Vehicles Rule, 1989 to ensure compliance.  \n \n(d) to (f):   As per information received from Ministry of Road Transport & Highways \n(MoRTH), expert c ommittee was constituted with independent experts from DRDO, Indian \nInstitute of Science (IISC) Bengaluru and Naval Science & Technological Laboratory (NSTL) \nVisakhapatnam. \n \n \n The following manufacturers have recalled vehicles: \n \ni. Okinawa has recalled 3215 units of vehicles on 16th April, 2022. \nii. Pure EV has recalled 2000 units of vehicles on 21st April, 2022. \niii. Ola Electric has recalled 1441 units of vehicles on 23rd April, 2022. \n  \n Based on the information of fire incidents available, MoRTH has i ssued a show cause \nnotice to the CEOs and MDs of the concerned two wheeler electric scooter manufacturers, to \nexplain the reasons as to why the relevant sections of the Motor Vehicles Act should not be \ninvoked against them. \n \n******* \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_235": "Government of India \nMinistry of Youth Affairs & Sports \nDepartment of Sports \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *235 \nTO BE ANSWERD ON 02.08.2022 \n \nFunctioning of SAI Centres \n \n*235. SHRI. RAMESH CHANDRA MAJHI: \n \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) the details of the total number of Sports Authority of India (SAI) \ncentres functioning and the total number of sportspersons being \nimparted training in these centres in the country;  \n \n(b) whether the SAI has established adequate inf rastructure to \nprovide proper training facilities to the sportspersons with a view to \npromote sports activities in the country;  \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof and the steps taken/ being taken by the \nGovernment presently along with achievements made and l ong term \nplan evolved, State/ UT-wise including Odisha; \n \n(d) whether the Government has any proposal to increase SAI centres \nfor increasing the seats for sportspersons at all such centres in the \ncountry particularly in Odisha; and  \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n[SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR] \n \n(a) to (e) A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n***** \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO ( e) OF THE  \nLOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. *235 FOR REPLY ON 02.08.2022 \nASKED BY SHRI RAMESH CHANDRA MAJHI REGARDING \n“FUNCTIONING OF SAI CENTRES” \n \n(a) A total of 189 Centres, including National Centres of Excellence \n(NCOEs), SAI Training Centres (STCs) and their Extension Centres are \nfunctional for implementation of the sports promotional schemes of \nSAI. Presently, a total of 8119 talented sports persons (4973 boys and \n3146 girls) are being trained in 28 sports disciplines on residential and \nnon-residential basis in these Centres. \n \n(b) and (c) ‘Sports’ being a State subject, the primary responsibility of \ndevelopment of sports infrastructure lies with the State /UT \nGovernments. However, the Government of India has tak en various \nsteps to strengthen s ports infrastructure through Khelo India \nScheme, Assistance to National Sports Federations , National Sports \nDevelopment Fund Scheme and by providing facilities in various SAI \ncentres. \nUnder the vertical “Utilization and Creation of Sports Infrastructure” \nof the Khelo India Scheme, Central Government has sanctioned three \nhundred (300) sports infrastructure projects of various types across \nthe country , including four (4) in the state of Odisha . State-wise \ndetails of the projects are given at Annexure-I. \n \nBesides, the sports infrastructure facilities available with SAI have \nsufficient capacity to cater to the training needs of a large number of \nathletes, including high performance training to elite athletes across \nthe country at the SAI NCOE s. The details  of the existing \ninfrastructure are given at Annexure-II.   \n \n(d) & (e) SAI has recently undertaken an exercise of restructuring \nof its existing sports promotional schemes. In view of the available \nresources, it has been decided to first focus on the existing centres \nin identified sports disciplines  in order to prepare athletes for major \ninternational events like the Asian Games, the Commonwealth \nGames, the Olympic Games , etc. Moreover, the creation and \ndevelopment of sports infrastructure is a dynamic process. Therefore, \n\nthe decision of creation of facilities/SAI centres depends on various \nfactors such as availability of funds, local demand, etc.  \n \nCurrently, SAI runs one (01) National Centre of Excellence, three (03) \nSAI Training Centres, two (02) Extension Centres, one (01) Regional \nSchool and one (0 1) Akhara  in the state of Odisha. However, at \npresent, there is no such proposal under consideration for Odisha. \n \n*****  \n\nANNEXURE–I \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART S (b) AND (c) OF THE \nLOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. *235  FOR 02.08.2022 ASKED \nBY SHRI RAMESH CHANDRA MAJHI REGARDING “FUNCTIONING OF \nSAI CENTRES” \nS. \nNo. State/UT No. of \nprojects \nAmount \nsanctioned \n(in crore) \nAmount \nreleased \n(in crore) \n1 Andaman and Nicobar Islands 1 7.23 0.00 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 7 33.80 17.04 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 21 183.72 117.86 \n4 Assam 8 47.68 42.68 \n5 Bihar 11 50.83 20.50 \n6 Chandigarh 0 0.00 0.00 \n7 Chhattisgarh 4 20.65 8.77 \n8 Dadra & Nagar Haveli and Daman & Diu 0 0.00 0.00 \n9 Delhi 8 89.36 46.78 \n10 Goa 4 19.10 2.12 \n11 Gujarat 5 608.37 304.63 \n12 Haryana 10 88.89 49.03 \n13 Himachal Pradesh 8 38.10 5.85 \n14 Jammu and Kashmir 5 27.89 16.67 \n15 Jharkhand 2 10.38 2.07 \n16 Karnataka 19 128.52 57.42 \n17 Kerala 5 62.74 28.12 \n18 Ladakh 3 14.28 12.33 \n19 Lakshadweep 2 9.00 0.00 \n20 Madhya Pradesh 12 85.64 35.37 \n21 Maharashtra 13 110.80 64.39 \n22 Manipur 8 80.45 39.03 \n23 Meghalaya 6 28.00 5.00 \n24 Mizoram 7 39.00 24.48 \n25 Nagaland 7 45.00 29.30 \n26 Odisha 4 28.00 23.38 \n27 Puducherry 3 16.02 5.00 \n28 Punjab 11 93.71 51.28 \n29 Rajasthan 45 112.26 76.51 \n30 Sikkim 5 25.83 12.18 \n31 Tamil Nadu 5 33.00 10.00 \n32 Telangana 6 24.11 16.70 \n33 Tripura 7 38.35 19.57 \n34 Uttar Pradesh 32 502.52 147.32 \n35 Uttarakhand 5 23.78 21.14 \n36 West Bengal 1 26.77 12.41 \n  Total 300 2753.78 1324.93 \n\nANNEXURE–II \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (b) AND (c) OF THE \nLOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. *235  FOR 02.08.2022 ASKED \nBY SHRI RAMESH CHANDRA MAJHI REGARDING “FUNCTIONING OF \nSAI CENTRES” \n \nDETAILS OF EXISTING INFRASTRUCTURE: SAI REGIONAL CENTRE-WISE \nSl.No. \nName Of \nRegional \nCentre \nDetails of Fields of Play \nDetails of  \nExisting \nSports \nHostels \n1 RC- LNCPE \nTrivandrum \nCycling Velodrome, Swimming pool, Four multipurpose \nindoor Sports halls, Two multipurpose Play fields, 400 \nSynthetic Athletic Track ,  Two 400 m Clay Tracks Boat \nHouse and open hall, Synthetic hockey Pitch and Multiple