    "ls|LS17_S9_U_49": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 49 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2022 \n \nTRANSPORTATION OF COAL \n \n* 49.  SHRI BRIJBHUSHAN SHARAN SINGH: \n SHRI P.P. CHAUDHARY: \n \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the current status of rakes deployed for transportation of coal to \npower plants across the country; \n \n(b) whether the Government has taken measures to deploy additional \nrakes to augment transportation of coal to power plants in the \nlight of recent developments and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether the Government plans to or has undertaken measures to \nupgrade coal rake wagons to incorporate upsurge in demand; \n \n(d) whether the Government has envisaged a role for ‘Startups for \nRailways’ policy to constructively resolve the issue and if so, the \ndetails thereof; and  \n \n(e) the details regarding distribution of coal for this financial year to \nthe States? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND  \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n(a) to (e) : A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n***** \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 49 BY SHRI BRIJBHUSHAN SHARAN SINGH  AND SHRI \nP.P. CHAUDHARY ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 20.07.2022 REGARDING \nTRANSPORTATION OF COAL \n \n(a) & (b) : Considering the importance of coal transportation, Indian \nRailways has increased the deployment of rakes for coal loading to \npower houses.  D uring, first quarter of current financial year (April -June \n2022), Railways have deployed average 504 rakes per day for \ntransportation of coal to power plants across the country. Against this, \nthe coal loading to power sector during the period has been 434 rakes \nper day which is 32% more than the corresponding period of 2021 -22. In \nJuly 2022 (upto 13 th), Railways have deployed 504 rakes for \ntransportation of coal to power plants against which coal loading to \npower sector has been 425 rakes per day which is 41% more than the \ncorresponding period in July 2021.   \n \n(c): Besides augmenting additional rakes, Indian Railways has \nundertaken measures to deploy higher capacity wagons (BOXNS type) for \ncoal loading on routes which are fit for higher axle load operations and \nwhich have additional pay load of 13% over normal open wagons to meet \nthe enhanced demand.  \n \n(d): No, Sir. \n \n(e): In 2022 -23, Coal India Limited(CIL) has Annual Contracted Quantity \n(ACQ) under Fuel Supply Agreement (FSA) with State Power Gencos for a \nquantity   of  188.7  Million  Tonnes(MT).   Apart  from  this,  coal  is  being  \n \nContd….2/- \n\n: 2 : \nallocated to State Nominated Agencies of the concerned State(s) for \ncurrent Financial Year ( FY), to be supplied through FSA. Singareni \nCollieries Company Limited (SCCL) has plans to dispatch 70 MT during \nthe year 2022-23, out of which 57.8 MT is for power sector. \nThe State wise details of distribution of coals are as under: \n(in Million Tonnes) \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n***** \nState(s) FSA Quantity \nof the \nConcerned \nState \nGencos \nAllocation of \nCoal to State \nNominated \nAgencies of \nthe Concerned \nState(s) \nSCCL \nto \nPower \nsector \nCIL \nAndhra Pradesh 20.11 - 6.192 \nBihar - 0.14 - \nChhattisgarh 9.61 0.06 0.2 \nGujarat 14.02 0.09 - \nJharkhand 2 0.22 - \nJ&K - 0.01 - \nHaryana 10.88 0.01 - \nKarnataka 5.12 - 12.36 \nMadhya Pradesh 23.22 0.04 - \nMaharashtra 34.06 0.49 4.465 \nNagaland - 0.01 - \nOdisha 2.7 0.04 - \nPunjab 4.12 0.02 - \nRajasthan 16.71 0.02 - \nTamil Nadu 19.04 - 2.8 \nTripura - 0.02 - \nUttar Pradesh 23.8 0.28 - \nWest Bengal 3.32 0.1 1.0 \nTelangana - - 30.79 ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_5": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.5 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.07.2022 \n \nMONUMENTS OF NATIONAL IMPORTANCE IN JHARKHAND. \n \n5. DR. NISHIKANT DUBEY: \nWill the Minister of Culture be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the number of monuments or sites declared as monument s of national \nimportance by the Archaeological Survey of India (ASI), State- wise including \nJharkhand; \n(b) the details of the funds spent on the said monuments or sites during the last five \nyears; \n(c) the revenue ear ned by the Government from these monuments during each of \nthe last three years; \n(d) whether the Government proposes to declare any other monument as monument \nof national importance; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof, State-wise? \n \nANSWER \n \n                               THE MINISTER OF CULTURE, TOURISM AND \n                               DEVELOPMENT OF NORTH EASTERN REGION \n  \n                                              (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) There are 3 ,693 monuments and sites declared as of national importance \nthroughout India. State-wise details are at Annexure-I \n(b) The expenditure incurred on conservation and maintenance of these monuments \nduring the last five years is at Annexure-II.  \n(c) The revenue earned from sale of entry tickets at centrally protected ticketed \nmonuments during the last three years is as under: \nYear Revenue collected \n(amount in lakh rupees) \n2019-20 33623.23 \n2020-21 4032.18 \n2021-22 10150.59 \n \n(d) \n& \n(e) \nList of monuments and sites identified to declare them as of National Importance \nis at Annexure-III. \n \n \n \n \n\n \nANNEXURE-I \n \n               ANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED    QUESTION NO.  5 FOR 18.07.2022. \n \n          STATE-WISE NUMBER OF MONUMENTS/SITES OF NATIONAL \nIMPORTANCE IN THE  COUNTRY. \nSl.N\no. \nName of State / U.T. Number of \nMonuments/Site \n1.  Andhra Pradesh  135 \n2.  Arunachal Pradesh  03 \n3.  Assam  55 \n4.  Bihar  70 \n5.  Chhattisgarh  46 \n6.  Daman & Diu (U.T.)  11 \n7.  Goa  21 \n8.  Gujarat  203 \n9.  Haryana  91 \n10.  Himachal Pradesh  40 \n11.  Jammu & Kashmir (U.T.)  56 \n12.  Jharkhand  13 \n13.  Karnataka 506 \n14.  Kerala  29 \n15.  Ladakh (U.T.)  15 \n16.  Madhya Pradesh 291 \n17.  Maharashtra 286 \n18.  Manipur  01 \n19.  Meghalaya  08 \n20.  Mizoram  01 \n21.  Nagaland  04 \n22.  N.C.T. Delhi 173 \n23.  Odisha  80 \n24.  Puducherry (U.T.)  07 \n25.  Punjab  33 \n26.  Rajasthan 163 \n27.  Sikkim 03 \n28.  Telangana  08 \n29.  Tamilnadu 412 \n30.  Tripura  08 \n31.  Uttar Pradesh 743 \n32.  Uttarakhand  43 \n33.  West Bengal 135 �\nTOTAL 3693 ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_50": "��\n��\nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO- 50 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON- 18/07/2022 \n  \nSCHEDULED TRIBES COMPONENT \n \n50. SHRIMATI RAKSHA NIKHIL KHADSE:  \n \nWill the Minister of TRIBAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has any mechanism put in place to monitor the Scheduled Tribe Component \n(STC) and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(b) the details of the manpower that has been sanctioned in the form of sanctioned posts for the same; and \n \n(c)  the details of the filled up and vacant posts till date? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TRIBAL AFFAIRS \n(SMT. RENUKA SINGH SARUTA) \n  \n(a): The Ministry of Tribal Affairs is the nodal Ministry for overall policy, planning and coordination of \nprogrammes of development for the Scheduled Tribes (STs). However, with regard to sectoral \nprogrammes and schemes of development of ST communities, policy, planning, monitoring, evaluation, \netc., as also their coordination are the responsibility of the concerned Central Ministries / Departments, \nState Governments and Union Territory Administrations. NITI Aayog has issued guidelines for \nearmarking, effective utilization of Tribal Sub Plan (TSP)/ Scheduled Tribe Component \n(STC)/Development Action Plan for STs (DAPST) funds by the obligated Ministries/Departments for the \nwelfare and development of STs.  \n  \nMinistry of Tribal Affairs has developed STC MIS Portal with web address: https://stcmis.gov.in for \nmonitoring of TSP/STC/DAPST funds of the obligated Ministries/Departments. The Ministry also takes \nmeetings periodically with the obligated Ministries/Departments to review allocation, utilization, and \nphysical progress under TSP/STC/DAPST. Adherence of TSP/STC allocation earmarking norm as per \nguidelines, identification of schemes, allocation and utilization of TSP/STC funds under such schemes \nwhich provide specific benefits to STs, setting up of target/deliverables under TSP and monitoring of \nprogress, etc., are emphasized in the review meetings. Physical progress under TSP/STC is also obtained \nfrom the Ministries/Departments. \n \n(b) & (c): Department of Expenditure has sanctioned seven posts (one post of Director/Deputy Secretary, \ntwo posts of Under Secretary, two posts of Section Officer and two posts of Assistant Section Officer) in \nthe Ministry of Tribal Affairs for TSP/STC monitoring. Out of the seven posts sanctioned, one post of \nDirector/Deputy Secretary is filled. \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_51": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 51 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2022 \n \nSURVEY FOR NEW RAILWAY LINES \n \n*51. SHRI RAJENDRA DHEDYA GAVIT: \nSHRI UNMESH BHAIYYASAHEB PATIL: \n \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of survey approved for new railway lines in the country \nduring the last three years and the current year along with th e \npresent status thereof, zone/ section-wise particularly in the Lok \nSabha constituencies of Kutch, Arambagh, Jalgaon, Palghar and \nBhadohi;  \n \n(b) the details of funds allocated and spent on these surveys during \nthe last three years, zone-wise;  \n \n(c) whether the Railways has sanctioned projects for laying of new \nrailway lines for which surveys have already been completed, if \nso, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor, \nzone/section-wise; \n \n(d) whether any time frame has been fixed to reach a final decision \non the completed survey reports for laying of new railway lines \nand if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(e) the steps taken/being taken by the Government to expedite the \nprojects for which surveys have already been completed? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND  \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n(a) to (e): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n*****  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) T O (e) OF \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 5 1 BY SHRI RAJENDRA DHEDYA GAVIT AND \nSHRI UNMESH BHAIYYASAHEB PATIL TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK \nSABHA ON 20.07.2022 REGARDING SURVEY FOR NEW RAILWAY LINES \n \n(a): New Railway line projects are surveyed/ sanctioned Zonal \nRailway wise and not Parliamentary constituency wise  as the \nRailways’ surveys/projects may span across State/UT boundaries.  \nIn the last three years i.e. 2019 -20, 2020 -21, 2021 -22 and current \nFinancial year 2022 -23, 104 surveys for New Lines of a total length of \n7652 km have been approved throughout the country includ ing those \nfalling fully/partly in parliamentary constituencies of Kutch, Arambagh, \nJalgaon, Palghar and Bhadohi. Zonal Railway wise details of these \nsurveys are given below:- \n \nSN Zone No. of New line \nsurveys approved. \nTotal Length  \n(in km) \n1 Central Railway 6 784 \n2 East Coast Railway 14 923 \n3 East Central Railway 10 638 \n4 Eastern Railway 3 119 \n5 North Central Railway 2 255 \n6 North Eastern Railway 3 241 \n7 Northeast Frontier \nRailway \n6 584 \n8 Northern Railway 16 556 \n9 North Western Railway 8 795 \n10 South Central Railway 1 75 \n \n..P/2 \n\n \n-:2:- \n11 South East Central \nRailway \n10 1232 \n12 South Eastern Railway 6 388 \n13 Southern Railway 2 135 \n14 South Western Railway 6 417 \n15 West Central Railway 4 95 \n16 Western Railway 7 415 \n \n(b): The details of funds all ocated Zonal Railway wise  surveys taken \nup during the last three years i.e. Financial Year 2019 -20 to 2021 -22 \nand funds allocated in the  current Financial Year 2022 -23 alongwith \nexpenditure incurred on surveys during the last three Financial Years \ni.e. 2019-2020, 2020-21, 2021-22 are given below:- \n \nSN Railway Funds \nallocated for \nsurveys \n(Revised \nEstimate) in \nlast 03 years \n2019-20 to \n2021-22   \n(` in crore) \n*Expenditure \nincurred on \nsurveys in the \nlast 03 years \n2019-20,2020-21 \nand     2021-22 \n(` in crore) \nFunds \nallocated for \nsurveys in FY \n2022-23 \n(Budget \nEstimate) \n(` in crore) \n1 Central 17.63 6.15 6.29 \n2 East Central 23.70 2.56 0.56 \n3 East Coast  4.07 0.38 3.59 \n4 Eastern 1.97 0.73 1.00 \n \n..P/3 \n \n\n-:3:- \n5 North Central 5.10 1.27 1.07 \n6 North Eastern 10.68 1.68 10.13 \n7 North Western 13.91 8.56 3.81 \n8 Northeast \nFrontier \n12.98 12.15 6.30 \n9 Northern 106.97 73 7.60 \n10 South Central 14.30 5.69 7.72 \n11 South East \nCentral \n3.99 0.90 1.84 \n12 South Eastern 10.69 4.92 6.83 \n13 \nSouth Western \n5.91 2.65 2.46 \n14 Southern 9.99 4.81 4.29 \n15 West Central 3.16 1.02 0.46 \n16 Western 9.70 4.93 3.54 \n(*Includes actual expenditure for FY 2019 -20, 2020-21 and provisional  \nexpenditure for 2021-22) \n \n(c): Yes, Sir. Out of the surveys completed in the last 03 years i.e.   FY \n2019-20, 2020-21, 2021-22 and the current Financial year, 09 New Line \nprojects have been sanctioned. Zonal Railway wise details are as \nunder:- \nRailway No. of new line projects \nsanctioned in the last three \nyears and current year. \nTotal length \n(in kms) \nCentral 01 108 \nEast Coast 01 14 \nNorth Eastern  01 81 \n \n..P/4 \n\n-:4:- \n \nNorth Western 02 59 \nNortheast Frontier 01 2.7 \nSouth Western 03 194 \n \n(d): Detailed Project Report (DPR) is prepared for those projects which \nare fou nd feasible after survey . Projects are appraised based on \nparameters brought out in DPR. After firming up of various parameters, \nfurther sanctioning of project requires consultation with various state -\nholders including State Governments and necessary approvals viz. \nappraisal of NI TI Aayog., approval of Cabinet Committee on Economic \nAffairs etc. As sanctioning of projects is a continuous and dynamic \nprocess, exact timelines cannot be fixed.  \n(e): Various steps taken by the Government for speedy sanction and \nimplementation of rail projects include (i) setting up of Gati Shakti \nunits (ii) prioritisation of projects (iii) substantial increase in allocation \nof funds on priority projects (iv) delegation of powers at field level (v) \nclose  monitoring  of  progress  of project at various levels, and (vi) \nregular follow up with State Governments and concerned authorities \nfor expeditious land acquisition, forestry and Wildlife clearances and \nfor resolving other issues pertaining to projects. This has led to \nsubstantial increase in rate of commissioning since 2014. \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_52": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 52 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.7.2022 \n \nPension Rules for JNV Employees \n \n52. SHRI RAJMOHAN UNNITHAN: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government noticed that Jawahar Navodaya Vidyalayas employees/ \nStaffs who have joined on or before 1 January 2004 are not entitled to get a CCS \npension (1972) as per rules; and \n \n(b)  if so, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n(SMT. ANNPURNA DEVI) \n \n(a) & (b) : Department of Pension and Pensioners’ Welfare issued an Office \nMemorandum dated 01.05.1987 whereby all Contributory Provident Fund (CPF) \nbeneficiaries, who were in service as on 01.01.1986 would be deemed to have come \nover to the Pension Scheme, unless specifically opted to remain under the CPF \nScheme. The employees of NVS had been given the benefits of CPF Scheme w.e.f. \n01.04.1988. The matter to extend the benefit of CCS (Pension) Ru les, 1972 to NVS \nemployees who joined prior to 01.01.2004 has been considered by the Ministry from \ntime to time  in consultation with Ministry of Finance and Department of Pension and \nPensioners’ Welfare. H owever, it has not been agreed to, as it would be a gainst the \npolicy regime of the Government of India  in terms of Department of Expenditure’s \ndirectives dated 16.03.2000. The New Pension Scheme (now National Pension System) \n(NPS), which was introduced for the Central Government employees w.e.f. 01.01.2004 , \nwas made applicable to the regular employees of NVS w.e.f.  01.04.2009. Those \nemployees who had joined NVS on regular basis before 01.04.2009 were given an \noption to continue with the existing CPF Scheme or to join the NPS.  Those employees \nof NVS who have opted for and are covered under the NPS, are entitled to the benefit s \nenvisaged under this Scheme. The staff of NVS are entitled to benefits of either the \nCPF Scheme or the NPS Scheme having regard to the option exercised by them. \n \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_53": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS   \nDEPARTMENT OF TELECOMMUNICATIONS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 53 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20TH JULY, 2022 \n \n \nPENALTY FOR CALL DROPS \n \n†*53. SHRI GOPAL CHINNAYA SHETTY:  \n \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Telecom Regulatory Authority of India (TRAI) has taken any decision in \nOctober, 2015 to impose penalty on all telecom companies for call drops with effect from January \n1, 2016;  \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c)  the amount of compensations per call drop which the telecom companies have to pay on \ncall drop under this new system; and  \n \n(d)  whether there is also a provision for giving information of compensation to the consumers \nby the telecom companies and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \n MINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS &  \nELECTRONICS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n(a) to (d)  A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n\nSTATEMENT TO BE LAID ON THE TABLE OF THE LOK SABHA IN RESPECT OF \nPARTS (a) TO (d) OF THE LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 53 FOR 20TH JULY,  \n2022 REGARDING ‘PENALTY FOR CALL DROPS’. \n \n(a) to (d)  Telecom Regulatory Authority of India (TRAI), vide Telecom Consumers \nProtection (Ninth Amendment) Regulations, 2015 dated 16.10.2015, mandated that every \noriginating service provider providing Cellular Mobile Telephone Service shall, for each call drop \nwithin its network- \ni. credit the account of the calling consumer by one rupee:  \nProvided that such credit in the account of the calling consumer shall be limited to three \ndropped calls in a day (00:00:00 hours to 23:59:59 hours);  \nii. provide the calling consumer, through SMS/USSD message, within four hours of the \noccurrence of call drop, the details of amount credited in his account; and  \niii. in case of post-paid consumers, provide the details of the credit in the next bill. \n \nThe service providers and their association filed a writ petition on 09.12.2015 before \nHon’ble High Court of Delhi against the afore -mentioned regulations. Hon’ble High Court of \nDelhi in judgement dated 29.02.2016 upheld the validity of the regulations. Subsequently, the \nservice providers and their association through a special leave petition had appealed before \nHon’ble Supreme Court against the judgment of Hon’ble High Court of Delhi. Hon’ble Supreme \nCourt set aside the afore-mentioned regulations vide its order dated 11th May 2016. \n************ ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_54": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.54 \nANSWERED ON 18.07.2022 \n \nCREATION OF EMPLOYMENT AT TOURIST DESTINATIONS \n \n54. SHRI SHA.BRA.DR.JAI SIDDESHWAR SHIVACHARYA MAHASWAMIJI: \nSHRI S. MUNISWAMY:  \nDR. UMESH G. JADHAV:  \nSHRI SANGANNA AMARAPPA:  \nSHRI ANNASAHEB SHANKAR JOLLE:  \nSHRI B.Y. RAGHAVENDRA:  \nSHRI PRATHAP SIMHA: \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has taken any note of large-scale scope for \ncreation of employment at tourist destinations;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof, including the names of tourist destinations \nwhere the local people have really been benefitted out of the \ninitiative taken by Government;  \n(c) whether the Government has any plan to identify 5 to 10 new tourist \ndestinations in each State of the country which could be promoted \nand developed as potential tourist destinations with basic tourism \nfacilities based upon specially drawn -up master plans in the next \nfive years; and  \n(d) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM                                 (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a)&(b): Yes, Sir. The Ministry of Tourism started the Destination \nBased Skill Development training programme at 7 iconic sites i.e. Taj \nMahal at Agra, Humayun Tomb, Red Fort, Qutub Minar at Delhi, \nMahabodhi Temple at Bihar, Colva Beach at Goa and Kaziranga at \nAssam in Financial Year 2019 -20 to train, upgrade and sensitize the \nlocal people residing near these tourism sites and destinations. \n  \nThis programme continued in the  Financial Year 2020 -21 and \nupscaled to more than 150 new destinations. However, due to Covid -19 \n\npandemic this programme took place at 44 destinations. The list of \ndestinations is given at Annexure-I.  \n \nThis programmewas continued during the Financial Year 2021 -22 \nand conducted at 48 destinations. The list of destinations is given at \nAnnexure-II. \n \nThe Ministry of Tourism conducts online the Incredible India Tourist \nFacilitator Certification (IITFC). In these courses basic, advance (heritage \nand adventure), spoken language and refresher courses are taught. So far \na total of 3795 candidates have been successfully certified. \n \n(c)&(d):     Development and identification of new tourism spots, is \nprimarily the responsibility of the concerned State Government/ Union \nTerritory Administration and this is a continuous process.  Ministry of \nTourism, Government of India under its Schemes of Swadesh Darshan, \nPRASHAD and Assistance to Central Agencies, provides central financial \nassistance to State Governments/Union Territories/ Central Agencies for \ndevelopment of tourism related infrastructure and facilities to provide an \nenriching tourism experience to visitors. The projects under the Schemes \nare identified for development in consultation with State Governments/ \nUT Administration s and are sanctioned subject to availability of funds, \nsubmission of suitable detailed project reports, adherence to scheme \nguidelines and utilization of funds released earlier. \n \nThe Ministry of Tourism revamped its Swadesh Darshan scheme as \nSwadesh Darshan 2.0 (SD2.0) with objective to develop sustainable and \nresponsible destinations following a tourist & destination centric \napproach. \n \n******* \n\nANNEXURE-I \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) & (b) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 54 ANSWERED ON 18.07.2022 R EGARDING CREATION OF \nEMPLOYMENT AT TOURIST DESTINATIONS  \n \nS.No Name of Destination State \n1 Malanithan Arunachal Pradesh \n2 Doorjeeling Arunachal Pradesh \n3 Ziro, Lower Subansiri Arunachal Pradesh \n4 Namsai, Namsai District Arunachal Pradesh \n5 Kaziranga Assam \n6 Kamakhaya Temple Assam \n7 Vaishali Bihar \n8 Agrasen Ki Baoli Delhi \n9 Brahma Sarovar Haryana \n10 Sheikh Chilli's Tomb Haryana \n11 Rock-Cut Temple Himachal Pradesh \n12 Macloadganj Himachal Pradesh \n13 Bhedaghat Jabalpur \n14 Patratu Jharkhand \n15 Mangolia sunset point Jharkhand \n16 Hampi Karnataka \n17 SreePadmanabaswamy Temple Kerala \n18 Bekal Fort Kerala \n19 Gwalior Fort Madhya Pradesh \n20 Orchcha Madhya Pradesh \n21 Pre-historic rock shelters of Bhimbetka Madhya Pradesh \n22 Panna Madhya Pradesh \n\n23 Elephanta Caves Maharashtra \n24 Cherrapunjee Meghalaya \n25 Living Root Bridges Meghalaya \n26 Falkland, Aizwal Mizoram \n27 PhawngpuiTlang (Lawngtlai) Mizoram \n28 \nLianchhiariLunglenTlang, Dungtlang \n(Champhai) \nMizoram \n29 Reiek (Mamit) Mizoram \n30 Anandpur Sahib Punjab \n31 Goldern Temple Amritsar Punjab \n32 Amber Fort Rajasthan \n33 Kailash Nath Temple, Kanchipuram Tamil Nadu \n34 Mahabalipuram Tamil Nadu \n35 Rameshwaram Tamil Nadu \n36 Ramappa Temple,Warangal Telangana \n37 Golconda Fort, Hyderabad Telangana \n38 Vaishnodevi (Katra) UT of Jammu & Kashmir \n39 Bhaderwah UT of Jammu & Kashmir \n40 Pahalgam UT of Jammu & Kashmir \n41 Leh Palace UT of Ladakh \n42 Taj Mahal, Agra Uttar Pradesh \n43 Badrinath Uttarakhand \n44 Gangotri Uttarakhand \n \n****** \n\nANNEXURE-II \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) & (b) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO.  54 ANSWERED ON 18.07.2022 REGARDING CREATION OF \nEMPLOYMENT AT TOURIST DESTINATIONS  \n \nS.No Name of Destination State \n1 Lord Venkateswara Temple Andhra Pradesh \n2 Kaziranga Assam \n3 Kamakhya Temple Assam \n4 HariharNath Temple, Sonepur, \nBihar Bihar \n5 Macloadganj Himachal Pradesh \n6 Rock Cut Gardan Himachal Pradesh \n7 Patratu, Jharkhand Jharkhand \n8 Netarhat Jharkhand \n9 Devari Temple Jharkhand \n10 Hampi Karnataka \n11 Sarvangolobela Karnataka \n12 The Mysore Palace AmbaVilasa Karnataka \n13 Neyyar, Trivananthapuram Kerala \n14 Ponmudi, Trivanathapuram Kerala \n15 Achankkoli Kollam Kerala \n16 Thekkady, Idukki Kerala \n17 Vagamon, Idukki Kerala \n18 Gavi, Idukki Kerala \n19 Orchcha Madhya Pradesh \n20 Bedaghat Madhya Pradesh \n21 Gwalior Fort Madhya Pradesh \n22 Satna Madhya Pradesh \n23 Panna Madhya Pradesh \n24 Chhatarpur Madhya Pradesh \n25 Bhojpur Madhya Pradesh \n26 Gateway of India Maharashtra \n27 Living Root Bridges Cherrapunjee Meghalaya \n28 Mawphlang Scared Grove Meghalaya \n29 Chandrabhaga, Konarak Orissa \n30 Sheesh Mahal, Patiala Punjab \n\n31 Qila Mubarak Punjab \n32 Mahabalipuram Tamilnadu \n33 Kailash Nath Temple, Kanchipuram Tamilnadu \n34 yadagirigutta temple Telengana \n35 Vaishnodevi (Katra) UT of Jammu & Kashmir \n36 Kupwara UT of Jammu & Kashmir \n37 Banihal UT of Jammu & Kashmir \n38 Bhaderwah UT of Jammu & Kashmir \n39 Mantalai UT of Jammu & Kashmir \n40 Pahalgam UT of Jammu & Kashmir \n41 Dal Lake, Srinagar UT of Jammu & Kashmir \n42 Leh Palace UT of Ladakh \n43 Chitrakoot Uttar Pradesh \n44 FatehpurSikri Uttar Pradesh \n45 Varansi Uttar Pradesh \n46 Mathura Uttar Pradesh \n47 Taj Mahal, Agra, UP Uttar Pradesh \n48 Gangotri Uttrakhand \n \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_55": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 55 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.07.2022 \n \nCONSERVATION OF EXCAVATION SITE IN KALABURGI \n55.  SHRI S. MUNISWAMY: \nDR. UMESH G. JADHAV:  \nWill the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to state:  \n(a) whether the Kanaganahalli and Sannati in Chittapur Taluka of Kalaburagi district in \nKarnataka represents one of the earliest stupa settlements in the country and is dated \nearlier than Sanchi Stupa;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof, including its historical and archaeological importance in the \nhistory of Buddhist excavations conducted in the early 1990s and 2000s by ASI which had \nresulted in interesting structural remains and archaeological findings;  \n(c) whether only a small portion of the excava tion done by the ASI is under the protection of \nthe Government and fairly a large part, prone to open under the bare sky, still unprotected;  \n(d) if so, the measures taken to conserve the excavation site by the ASI; and  \n(e) whether the Government has any plan to declare the ASI site as UNESCO World Heritage \nand if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF CULTURE, TOURISM AND DEVELOPMENT OF NORTH EASTERN \nREGION \n \n(SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a)  Kanaganahalli and Sannati in Chittapur Taluka of Kalaburagi District represents one of the \nearliest stupa settlements in the country which is contemporary to Sanchi stupa.  \n(b)  The site of Sannati  has four mounds which are archaeologically significant. The mounds \nare namely, Benagutti, Hasargundigi, Kanaganahalli and Ranamandla. The mound \nBenagutti is located on the left bank of Bhima basin in Chitapur Taluk. The mound yielded \nan elliptical brick v ihara of Satavahana period.  Ranamandla has an ancient fortification. \nRuins of a Buddhist Stupa, ayaka pillars, limestone railings with a panel depicting \nMauryan king Ashoka, standing and seated figures of Lord Buddha of 1st -2nd CE and \nother artefacts were  found at Kanaganahalli. The site was patronised mainly by the \nSatavahana dynasty.  \n(c) \n& \n(d) \nThe ancient site where excavations were conducted is protected. Architectural stone \nmembers are safely kept under roof shed. The conservation work of the main portion of \nStupa has been initiated. \n \n(e) There is no such proposal under consideration at present. \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_56": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 56 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.07.2022 \n \nDOMESTIC WORKERS \n \n56.    SHRI MADDILA GURUMOORTHY:  \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)whether there is any data on the total number of domestic workers \nin the country and if so, the number of such domestic workers who \nhave registered on the eshram portal so far; \n(b)whether the Government has launched any specific scheme or \nprogramme to alle viate the problems of the domestic workers \nespecially in light of the COVID19 pandemic;  \n(c)if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and  \n(d)whether it is true that a Draft National Policy for Domestic Workers \nis under consideration and  if so, the current status of the \nimplementation of the policy? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a):  As on 12th July, 2022, 2.76 crore domestic & household \nworkers have been registered on e-SHRAM portal. \n(b) & (c): The Unorganized Workers’ Social Security Act, 2008, now \nsubsumed in the Code on Social Security, 2020, provides for social \nsecurity to all unorganized workers including domestic workers. \nGovernment has launched Central Sector Schemes like Pradhan Mantri \nJeevan Jyoti Beema Yojana (PMJJBY) and Pradhan Mantri Suraksha \nBeema Yojana (PMSBY) for life and disability cover and Pradhan Mantri \nShram Yogi Man Dhan  Pension Yojana (PM -SYM) for pension to the \nunorganised workers including domestic workers. Domestic  Workers \nSector Skill Council  (DWSSC) under  Ministry of Skill Development and \nContd..2/- \n\n:: 2 :: \nEntrepreneurship is imparting various skill development training \nprogrammes to domestic workers. Ayushman Bharat Pradhan Mantri \nJan Arogya Yojana (PMJAY) pro vides secondary and tertiary health \nbenefits to all unorganized workers including domestic workers who \nare covered as eligible beneficiaries as per Socio Economic Caste \ncensus Data, 2011. The Labour Codes, viz Code on Wages, 2019, Code \non Occupational Safe ty, Health and Working Conditions, 2020 and \nSocial Security Code, 2020 provide for decent working conditions, \nwages,                    occupational Safety, grievances redressal \nmechanism and social security benefits to all categories of workers \nincluding domestic workers.  \n(d):  No, Sir. \n \n****** ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_57": "������������������� �\n���������������� �\n��������� �\n���������������������� �\n���������������������� �\n��\n������������������������������������ �\n��\n�������������������������������� �\n���������������������������������������� �\n��\n������������������������������������������������\n��\n���� ����������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���� �� ����������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������������������������\n���� ����������������������������������������������������\n�\n������ �\n�������������������������������������������������� �\n�������������������� �\n��\n������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n��\n� �\n\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������ ��� ����� ������ ��������� ������ ���� ����� ������� ������ ������� ������ ����\n������������\n��\n������ ����� ��������������� ��� ����� ���� ��������� ���������� ����� ������ ����� ���� ���� ���\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���� ��������� ���� ����������� ��������� ���� ����� ����������� ���� ����� ��� ������������ ���� ������\n�������������������������������������������\n��\n����������� ��� ��������� ��� ����������� ��� ��������� �������� ������ ���� ������ ������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������