��� ���� �������� ���� ��������\n���������� ����� ��������� ����� ��������� ����� ���������\n������������� ����� ��������� ����� ��������� ����� ����������\n������������������� ��������� ����� ��������� ������ ����������\n������������� ������ ���������� ����� ���������� ������ ����������\n����������� ���� �������� ���� �������� ��� �������\n���������������\n��\n����� ��������� ������ ���������� ������ ����������\n�������������������� ��������� ����� ��������� ����� ���������\n�������������\n��\n������ ���������� ������ ���������� ������ ����������\n������� ������� ����������������������� ����������������������� ����������������\n�\n�",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_71": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI, \nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION \nLOK  SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *71 \nANSWERED ON 21.07.2022 \n \nTAWI AND KOSI-MECHI RIVER PROJECTS \n†*71  SHRI JUGAL KISHORE SHARMA  SHRIMATI NAVNEET R AVI RANA \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI  be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the time by which the Tawi and Kosi-Mechi river projects are likely to be started and completed; \n(b) whether the Government is taking cognizance of the rivers and nallahs affected by these projects; \n(c) if so, the details thereof along with the measures taken for the same; \n(d) whether the Government is taking cognizance of the people likely to be affected who would require \nrehabilitation or to whom compensation is being deferred; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF JAL SHAKTI   \n \n(SHRI GAJENDRA SINGH SHEKHAWAT) \n \n(a) to (e) A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF STARRED QUESTION \nNO. *71 TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 21.07.2022 R EGARDING “TAWI AND KOSI-\nMECHI RIVER PROJECTS”  \n  \n(a)  The Kosi-Mechi intra-State link envisages diversio n of part of surplus water of river Kosi for \nextending irrigation in new command areas of Mahana nda basin lying in the districts of Bihar by \nextending existing Eastern Kosi Main Canal up to ri ver Mechi so that the rivers that flow through Biha r \ncould be linked together. The techno-economic clear ance and investment clearance of the project has \nbeen accorded by Department of Water Resources, Riv er Development and Ganga Rejuvenation. The \nclearances of Ministry of Environment, Forest and C limate Change (MoEFCC) have also been accorded \nto the project. Further, High Powered Steering Comm ittee of Government of India has recommended this \nproject for inclusion in the scheme of National Pro jects. The project is to be implemented by the Stat e \nGovernment of Bihar and completion period as per DPR of the project is five years.  \nThe Government of UT of Jammu & Kashmir has taken u p project viz. Development of Tawi Riverfront \nProject which involves construction of embankments with stepped retaining wall and diaphragm wall for \na length of 2.7 km on both sides of river. The project has completion period of eighteen months.  \n(b) & (c)  During planning stage itself and while formulation  of Detailed Project Report (DPR) of  river \nvalley project, cognizance of the rivers, nallahs, water bodies, etc. is taken into consideration and \nprovision for cross drainage structures such as sip hons, aqueducts, river over bridge is accordingly m ade \nin the project. This aspect has been duly considere d while according techno-economic clearance of Kosi -\nMechi intra-State Link. Jammu Smart City Limited, executing project on river Tawi, has informed that a ll \nthe drainage nallahs (3 on the right bank and 1 on the left bank) carrying rainwater and effluent lade n \nwater are planned to be diverted to STPs through in terceptor drains to be laid parallel to the flow on  both \nsides of the riverbanks. \n \n(d) & (e)  In respect of Kosi-Mechi link project, the State G overnment of Bihar has informed that \nprovisions of compensation to affected/ rehabilitat ed people is incorporated in the DPR of the project  as \nper the Right to Fair Compensation and Transparency  in Land Acquisition, Rehabilitation and \nResettlement Act, 2013. While for the Tawi river Pr oject, Jammu Smart City Limited executing project \nhas informed that this project is being executed in  the river basin entirely on the State Land and as such \nno private land is being acquired. There are 13 str uctures located on the left bank of river Tawi. Out  of \nthese 4 structures belong to the government agencie s and 9 structures belong to private individuals fo r \nwhich compensation as per norms is required to be paid.  \n  \n***  ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_72": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA  \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 72 \nANSWERED ON 21.07.2022 \n \nSOLAR PUMPS INSTALLED UNDER PM-KUSUM \n+*72.SHRIMATI DELKAR KALABEN MOHANBHAI \n       SHRI KRUPAL BALAJI TUMANE \n \nWill the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the number of solar pumps installed under the Pradhan Mantri Kisan Urja \nSuraksha evam Utthaan Mahabhiyan (PM-KUSUM) in Union Territories of \nDadra and Nagar Haveli, Daman and Diu and Maharashtra, especially in Nagpur-\nRamtek region;  \n(b)  the details of funds provided to the farmers for installation of solar pumps; \n(c)  the quantum of surplus electricity sold to the power distribution companies by the \nfarmers in the said State/UTs; \n(d)   the details of the size of agricultural land of farmers who installed such solar \npumps under the PM-KUSUM; \n(e)  the details of the ‘dark zones’ identified under the PM-KUSUM along with the \ncriteria laid down for the identification of such zones, State/UT-wise and the \nnumber of solar pumps installed/converted in the said ‘dark zones’; and \n(f)   the quantum of electricity generated by the farmers in these zones in the said \nState/UTs along with the revenue earned therefrom? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY \nAND  CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS \n \n(SHRI BHAGWANTH KHUBA)   \n(a) to (f) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n***** \n  \n\n \n \nStatement referred to in reply to parts (a) to (f) of Lok Sabha Starred Question \nNo. 72 (Priority No. 12) to be answered on 21.07.2022 regarding Solar Pumps \nInstalled under PM-KUSUM \n(a) to  (f) Under PM -KUSUM Scheme support for solar pumps is provided under \nComponent-B and Component -C. Under Component -B, support is provided for \ninstallation of standalone solar pumps. Under Component -C, support is provided for \nsolarization of existing grid -connected agriculture pumps, including agriculture feeder \nsolarization.  \nPM-KUSUM is a demand driven Scheme and therefore quantities of solar pumps are \nallocated to the States/ UTs based on demand received from the State Government/ UT \nAdministration. In absence of demand, solar pumps have not so far been  allocated to the \nUT of Dadra and Nagar Haveli and Daman and Diu. In the State of Maharashtra, 100,000 \nnos. of solar pumps have been sanctioned under the Component-B of the Scheme, out of \nwhich 7713 nos. of solar pumps have been installed as on 18.07.2022. Of these, 92 nos. \nof solar pumps have been installed in Nagpur and 11 nos. of solar pumps have been \ninstalled in Ramtek taluka of Nagpur.  \nUnder Component -B & C of the Scheme, 30% of the benchmark cost or t he price \ndiscovered through the tender, whichever is lower, is provided by the Central \nGovernment and at least an equivalent amount is provided by the concerned State \nGovernment. Till date, Ministry has released Rs. 672 Crore to various States for \ninstallation of standalone solar pumps.  \nAs solar pumps installed in the State of Maharashtra are off -grid i.e. these are not \nconnected to the grid, no electricity could be sold to the power distribution companies \nand, therefore, no revenue is earned by the farmer s through selling surplus electricity to \nthe power producing companies.  However, farmers save through avoided diesel \nconsumption by installing standalone solar pumps.  \nBased on the information provided by the State of Maharashtra, average land holding \nsize with farmers who have installed solar pumps under PM-KUSUM Scheme is 3.2 acre.  \nDark zones (areas with over-exploited ground water resources) are identified by Central \nGround Water Board (CGWB) in association with the State ground water organisations.  \nThe ground water resources are assessed in units i.e blocks/ talukas/ mandals/ \nwatersheds. These assessment units are categorized for ground water development based \non two criteria - a) stage of ground water development, and b) long -term pre and post \nmonsoon water levels. CGWB has notified 162 Over -exploited areas in the country for \nregulation of ground water development and management.  State/ UT-wise details of the \npumps installed/ converted in the dark zones is placed at Annexure. \n***** \n  \n\n \n \nAnnexure referred in reply to part (a) to (f) of the Lok Sabha Starred Question \nNo. 72 (Priority No. 12) to be answered on 21.07.2022 \n \nSolar Pumps Installed in Notified Areas of Central Ground Water Board (CGWB) \n \nS. No. State No. of Pumps Installed in \nNotified Areas \n1 Gujarat 135 \n2 Haryana 456 \n3 Madhya Pradesh  185 \n4 Punjab  642 \n5 Rajasthan 8165 \n6 Tamil Nadu 480 \nTotal 10063 \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_73": "Government of India \nMinistry of Development of North Eastern Region \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.73 \nTo be answered on \nMonday, July 18, 2022/Ashadha 27, 1944 (Saka) \nQUESTION \nDEVELOPMENT OF TOURIST LOCATIONS IN NER \n \n73.      SHRI ABDUL KHALEQUE: \nWill the Minister of DEVELOPMENT OF NORTH EASTERN REGION be pleased \nto state: \n(a)  whether the Government has any plans to develop  tourist locations in the North-\nEast Region (NER) particularly in Assam, and if so, the details thereof; \n(b)  whether the Government p roposes to consider  connecting important tourist \nlocations in NER, thus creating a tourism trail, if so, the details thereof; \n(c)  whether the Government has any development  plan centring around Dokreswar  \nDewalay, Chenga in  Barpeta District and Bageshwari Temple at Bongaigaon,  if so, the \ndetails thereof; and \n(d)  the efforts made/being made by the Government to promote the region abroad. \n \nANSWER \nThe Minister of  the Ministry of Development of  North Eastern Region \n[SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY] \n \n(a) & (b) The Ministry of Development of North Eastern Region (DoNER) under the \nNorth East Special Infrastructure Development Scheme (NESIDS) has  sanctioned \ninfrastructure projects relating to water supply, power, connectivity to support the  \ntourism sector in the north east region (NER) . Under NESIDS, 51 projects amounting to \n₹1,647 crore connecting tourist locations in the NER have been sanctioned . Of these, \n10 projects amounting to ₹637.14 crore are in the state of Assam. \nThe North Eastern Council (NEC), Shillong undertakes  activities for the  \npromotion of NER and regional tourism , which are priority sectors under the ‘Schemes \nof NEC’. Under this, the NEC has sanctioned 57 projects amounting ₹249.96 crore in \nthe tourism sector  in the last seven years . Further, 29 tourist circuits have been \n\nidentified by NEC and shared with the Ministry of Tourism. \nThe Ministry of Tourism  has sanctioned tourist circuit projects, amounting to \n₹1,254.07 crore in the NER under its ‘Swadesh Darshan’ scheme since 2014 -15. One \nproject has been sanctioned for Inland Waterways Authority of India (IWAI) under the \nscheme ‘Assistance to Central Agencies for Tourism Infrastructure Development ’ by \nthe Ministry of Tourism , for development of nine points of river cruise on national \nwaterways no. 1 & 2, out of which , four points, namely Neamati, Pandu, Jogighopa and \nBiswanath ghat are in Assam. Further, since 2014 -15 the Ministry of Tourism has \nprovided financial assistance to State Governments/Union Territory (UT) Administrators \nfor organizing fairs and festivals and tourism related events in the  NER. Under the \nCentral Sector Scheme of ‘National Mission on Pilgrimage Rejuvenation and Spiritual, \nHeritage Augmentation Drive (PRASHAD) ’, the Minist ry of Tourism funds projects for \nthe integrated development of identified pilgrimage and heritage destinations. As on \ndate 69 destinations/sites have been identified for development under the scheme in \n30 states/UTs. Of these, 20 sites are in the  NER, which include Kamakhya temple and \nSrikrishnaguru Sevashram, Nasatra in Assam. \n(c) No Sir, currently, the central government has no tourism development plans in \nthese areas. \n(d) India Tourism Offices Overseas along with Indian Missions abroad endeavour  to \nposition India as a preferred tourism destination and promote various tourism products \nand destinations including those in the NER and to increase India’s share in the global \ntourism market. The Ministry of Tourism under the Marketing Development Assis tance \n(MDA) programme extends financial support to approved tourism service providers and \nState Governments/UT Administra tors for undertaking tourism promotion in the \noverseas markets. On the request of the Ministry of Tourism, the Ministry of External \nAffairs has Tourism Officers in the Indian Missions of top 20 source markets in 2021. \nThe Ministry of Tourism has also conducted virtual road shows under the ‘Incredible \nIndia Reconnect 2022’ in Oman, United States of America, Germany, Australia, \nSingapore, T hailand, Spain and the United Kingdom to project India as a preferred \ntourism destination. \nVarious tourism promoting events and festivals etc. such as the ‘Sangai Festival’, \nManipur’, ‘Behdienkhlam Festival ’, Meghalaya’ and ‘Hornbilll Festival ’, Nagaland are \nsupported by NEC to attract tourists from different parts of the country as well as \nabroad.  \n**** ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_74": "Government of India \nMinistry of Finance \nDepartment of Expenditure \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 74 \n \nMONDAY, THE 18th July, 2022/Ashadha 27, 1944(Saka) \n \nRECOMMENDATION FOR SANCTIONING AIIMS \n \n74. ADV. ADOOR PRAKASH: \n \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Ministry has received any recommendation from the Ministry of Health \n& Welfare for sanctioning All India Institute of Medical Sciences (AIIMS) for Kerala; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government has considered the proposal for setting up of AIIMS in \nKerala; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof along with the decision taken thereon? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR FINANCE \n(SHRI PANKAJ CHAUDHARY) \n \n(a)-(b): Ministry of Finance had received proposal of the Ministry of Health & Family Welfare \nfor sanctioning an All India Institute of Medical Sciences (AIIMS) for Kerala. \n \n(c)-(d): AIIMS are set up under the Pradhan Mantri Swasthya Suraksha Yojana (PMSSY) \nimplemented by the Ministry of Health & Family Welfare. Government has approved setting \nup of AIIMS across the country in a phased manner. ",
