������ ��\n�\n\n��������\n����������������������������������������������������� ��������\n���������������������� ������������������������������������������������\n��������� ���� ��������� �������� ������� �������� ������� ������ �����\n���� ������ ������� ��� ������ ���� ��������� �������� ������� ����\n��������� ����������� ���� ���� ���������� �������� ���� �� ����� ���� ���\n����������������������������������������������������� ������������������\n���� ���� ��� ��� ������� ������ ���� ���������� ���� ������������� ��������\n�������������������������������������������� ������������������������\n������� ��� ���� ������� ����� ����� ���� ���� �� ��� ���� ��� ��������������\n������������������������������ ��������������������������������������\n���������� ���� ���� ������� ���� �� ������� ��� �������������� �������\n���������� ����� ���� ���� ��� ������� ���� ������ ���� ����� ���� �����������\n������ ����� ���� ��������� ���� �������� �� ����������� ��� �������� ��� ����\n������������ ���� ������ ��������� ������������� ��� ����� ��� ���� ��������\n���������������������������������������������������������������������\n��� ������������ �������������� ����� ����� ��������� ������ �� ����� ����\n���� �������� �������������� ���� �������� ��� ����� ���������� ����\n����������������������������������������������������������� �\n���������� ������ ����� ������ ��������� ����� �������� ������ �� �������\n������������� ������� ������ ��������� ����� ��������� ����� ��������\n������� ��������� ������ ����������� ���������� ����� ����� ������\n��������� ������� �������� �������� �������� ������� ����� ��������\n������������������������������������������������������������������ �\n�������� ������� ������� ����� �������� ����� ������ ���������\n�������������������������������������������������������������������\n���� ����� ���������� ��� ���� ������������ �������� ���������� ����� ������\n������������������������\n������������������������������������������ ������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������� �\n�\n������������������������������������������������������������\n� �\n\n�������� �\n�\n��������� �������� ��� ��� ������ ��� ����� ���� �� ����� ��� ���� ������ �� �\n�������� ��������� ��� ���� ���� ������������������������������� ���������������������������� ������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������� �\n������������������������������������\n�\n����������������\n����� ��������������� ������������\n�������� ������� �������\n�������� ������� �������\n�������� ������� �������\n�\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������\n����� ��������������� ������������\n�������� ������� �������\n�������� ������ �����\n�������� ����� �����\n�\n�������",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_96": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFAIRS\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO.96\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22.07.2022\nLOAN TO SRI LANKA\n*96.  SHRI RAMALINGAM S.:\nWill the Minister of EXTERNAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a) the total amount of various kind of short-term and long term loans\ngranted by the Union Government to the Government of Sri Lanka\nduring the last 10 years along with the terms and conditions;\n(b) whether the Government has extended any kind of help including\nfinancial assistance to Government of Sri Lanka to overcome the\nrecent economic crisis in Sri Lanka;\n \n(c) if so, the details thereof; and\n(d) the steps taken/proposed to be taken to recover the loan from the\nGovernment of Sri Lanka?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS\n(DR. SUBRAHMANYAM JAISHANKAR)\n(a) to (d) A statement is laid on the table of the House.\n1\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (d)  OF THE\nLOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION No. *96 REGARDING ‘LOAN TO\nSRI LANKA’ FOR ANSWER ON 22.07.2022.\n(a) to (d) Government of India has extended 08 Lines of Credit (LOCs) to Sri\nLanka  amounting  to  USD  1850.64  million  in  sectors  including  railways,\ninfrastructure, defence, renewable energy, petroleum and fertilizers in the past\n10 years. \nUnder  its  'Neighbourhood  First'  policy,  Government  is  committed  to\ndeveloping friendly and mutually beneficial relations with all its neighbours. In\nline  with  this  policy,  India  continues  to  assist  Sri  Lanka  in  its  economic\ndevelopment  as  also  support  it  in  overcoming  its  economic  challenges.  In\nJanuary 2022, India extended a US$ 400 million currency swap to Sri Lanka\nunder the SAARC Framework and deferred successive Asian Clearing Union\n(A.C.U.) settlements till July 6, 2022.  A Line of Credit of US$ 500 million was\nextended to Sri Lanka for importing fuel from India. In addition, India has\nextended  a  credit  facility  of  US$  1  billion  for  the  procurement  of  food,\nmedicines and other essential items from India. Humanitarian assistance was\nalso provided to Sri Lanka by gifting essential medicines worth about INR 6\ncrores, 15,000 litres of kerosene oil and US $ 55 million LoC for procurement\nof Urea fertilizer. Government of Tamil Nadu has contributed rice, milk powder\nand medicines worth US$ 16 million, as part of the larger Indian assistance\neffort.  \nDevelopment  assistance  under  Lines  of  Credit  (LoCs)  is  extended  in\naccordance  with the Indian  Development and  Economic Assistance  Scheme\n(IDEAS) guidelines of the Government of India.  In accordance with these\nguidelines,  the  terms  of  these  soft  loans  are  transparent,  with  low  rate  of\ninterest, moratorium in principal repayment, long repayment period and in-built\nflexibility.  \nIn case of the delay in repayment by borrowing Government, the issue is\ntaken up bilaterally.\n***\n2",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_97": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP  \n  \nLOK SABHA  \n  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 97  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.07.2022  \n  \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT TRAINING IN BIHAR  \n  \n97.  SHRI JANARDAN SINGH SIGRIWAL:  \n  \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRE NEURSHIP be \npleased to state:  \n  \n(a)   the details of the schemes/programmes being r un by the Government for Skill \nDevelopment training in all sectors through Skill Development in Bihar; \n \n(b)   the total number of candidates trained through the scheme during the last two years; \n \n(c)   if so, the details thereof, gender-wise; and \n \n(d)   the funds allocated and utilized for various projects under the scheme during each of \nthe last three years and the current year so far? \n  \nANSWER  \n  \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF  \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP  \n(SHRI RAJEEV CHANDRASEKHAR)  \n  \n(a) The Ministry of Skill Development and Entrepren eurship (MSDE) is delivering skill-\ntraining through a comprehensive network of skill d evelopment centres under various \nschemes viz. Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana (P MKVY), National Apprenticeship \nPromotion Scheme (NAPS), Jan Shikshan Sansthan (JSS ) and Craftsmen Training Scheme \n(CTS) through Industrial Training Institutes (ITIs)  for the unemployed youth in urban and \nrural areas across the country for unemployed youth  including the State of Bihar. Brief of \nthese schemes is as under: \n  \nPradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana (PMKVY):  The Ministry of Skill Development and \nEntrepreneurship (MSDE) is implementing its flagshi p, demand-driven, Centrally Sponsored \nScheme, PMKVY, for imparting short duration skill d evelopment training through Short-Term \nTraining (STT) and Recognition of Prior Learning (R PL) to youth across all States/ Union \nTerritories (UTs). \n  \nJan Shikshan Sansthan (JSS) Scheme: The Jan Sikshan Sansthan (JSS) initiative is a \nstep in the direction of realizing the unique demog raphic potential of urban slums and rural \nIndia. The programs are based on the polyvalent or multi-dimensional approach. The main \ntarget of the JSS is to impart vocational skills to  the non-literates, neo-literates and the \nperson having rudimentary level of education of 8 \nth  standard and school dropouts upto 12th \nstandard in the age group of 15-45 years, with due relaxation in case of “Divyangjan” and \nother deserving cases. Priority is given to Women, SC, ST, OBC and Minorities. More than \n50% beneficiaries of this scheme hail from rural areas. \n  \nNational Apprenticeship Promotion Scheme (NAPS):  This Scheme is for promoting \napprenticeship training and increasing the engageme nt of apprentices by providing financial \nsupport to industrial establishments undertaking ap prenticeship programme under the \nApprentices Act, 1961. Training consists of Basic T raining and On-the-Job Training / \nPractical Training at workplace in the industry. \n  \n\nCraftsmen Training Scheme (CTS) through Industrial Training Institutes (ITI): MSDE \nprovide long term training through ITIs. \n  \n(b)        The details of number of persons trained  in Bihar under the various schemes \nimplemented by MSDE during Financial Year 2020-21 and 2021-22 is as under: \n  \nS.No. \n Schemes  Number of people trained in \nBihar  \n1 Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana (PMKVY) 1,43, 931 \n2 Jan Shikshan Sansthan (JSS) 45,083 \n3 National Apprenticeship Promotion Scheme \n(NAPS) \n7,637 \n \n4 Craftsmen Training Scheme (CTS) 2,14,692 \n  \n(c)        The details of number of persons trained  in Bihar gender-wise under the various \nschemes implemented by MSDE during Financial Year 2020-21 and 2021-22 is as under: \n  \nS.No. \n Schemes  Male  Female  Transgender  \n1 Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana \n(PMKVY) \n91,491 52,438 2 \n2 Jan Shikshan Sansthan (JSS) 7,826 37,247 10 \n3 National Apprenticeship Promotion \nScheme (NAPS) \n5,743 1,894 -- \n4 Craftsmen Training Scheme (CTS) 2,01,717 12,975 - - \n  \n(d)        The details of funds allocated and utilized under these schemes are as under: \n  \nPMKVY : PMKVY has two components known as Centrally Spons ored Centrally Managed \n(CSCM) being implemented by MSDE through National S kill Development Corporation \n(NSDC) and Centrally Sponsored State Managed (CSSM)  being implemented by State Skill \nDevelopment Missions (SSDMs) of the States/ UTs. \n  \nUnder the CSSM component of PMKVY scheme, there is a provision of State/UT-wise \nallocation of funds, however under CSCM component, there is no provision for State-wise \nallocation of funds. \n  \nFunds allocated to the State of Bihar under CSSM Co mponent of PMKVY is as under which \nis fully utilized: \n  \nFinancial Year  2019-20  2020-21  2021-22  2022-23*  \nAmount (in Rs. Crore) 87.21 65.79 67.39 9.92 \n  \nJSS : Funds allocated to the State of Bihar under JSS is as under: \n  \nFinancial Year  2019-20  2020-21  2021-22  2022-23*  \nAmount (in Rs. Crore) 6.19 5.34 7.44 0 \n  \nNAPS : Funds disbursed to the State of Bihar under NAPS is as under: \n  \nFinancial Year  2019-20  2020-21  2021-22  2022-23*  \nAmount (in Rs. Crore) 0 1.15 1.02 0.26 \n  \n * Data till June, 2022  \n  \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_98": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.98 \nANSWERED ON 18.07.2022 \n \nCOMPETITIVE TOURISM RANKING SYSTEM \n \n98.  SHRI RANJEETSINGH HINDURAO NAIK NIMBALKAR:  \n       SHRI SUDHAKAR TUKARAM SHRANGARE:  \n       SHRI ARUN SAO:      \n \n       Will the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to introduce a competitive \nTourism Ranking System among cities across the country \nespecially in Maharashtra and Chhattisgarh States; \n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and \n(c) the steps taken by the Government to create, maintain and \npromote more and more tourist spots in Indian cities across the \ncountry as well as make more sites available for visit during \nnights? \nANSWER  \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM                               (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) & (b): Ministry of Tourism, Government of India has developed a \nTourist Destination Assessment Framework for evaluation of tourist \ndestination and their associated sites across the country.  \n \n(c): The Ministry of Tourism undertakes holistic promotion of various \ntourism destinations and products of the country. Identification and \ndevelopment of tourist sites including tourist sites available for visit \nduring nights is primarily the  responsibility of the respective State \nGovernment/Union Territory (UT) Administration. However, Ministry of \nTourism under the schemes of “Swadesh Darshan” and “National Mission \non Pilgrimage Rejuvenation and Spiritual, Heritage Augmentation Drive” - \nPRASHAD provides financial a ssistance to State Governments/UT \nAdministrations for development of tourism infrastructure, in \nconsultation with States/UTs. The provisions of proper lightings and \nillumination of destination is one of the most essential component under \nPRASHAD scheme wh ich makes site/ destination tourism friendly during \nthe night too . Further, financial assistance is also provided to various \ncentral agencies like Archaeological Survey of India, Port Trust of India, \nITDC, M inistry of Railways etc. for development of places of tourist \ninterest, illumination and preservation of monuments, development of \ncruise terminals etc. and to provide all infrastructure facilities required \nby the tourists. \n******* ",
